WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:05.096
(indistinct audience chattering)

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.883
(gentle music)

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.397
- So I've wanted to be a nurse since,

00:00:18.397 --> 00:00:21.330
I think I was about four years old.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:25.140
My dad actually had a
pretty serious back injury

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.310
when I was young,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.787
and so he spent a lot of time in the ICU

00:00:29.787 --> 00:00:34.560
and I remember the nurses there
made me feel so comfortable

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.060
and I realized, hey,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.240
I want to be the person to
make others feel comfortable.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.590
- Coming from an immigrant family,

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.710
I was really interested in really changing

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.910
how we talk about mental health

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:49.020
and also talking about the
opportunities that I have

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.080
to destigmatize mental
health in my community,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.610
so that was really important to me.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.450
- As a nurse, I get this
incredible privilege.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.400
It's completely transformed me.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.880
I've grown in confidence,
I've grown in self-awareness.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.960
I've grown to understand
what my purpose is

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.830
and the population in
which I want to practice.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.670
- Nursing allows us to have a unique

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.110
and intimate look into people's lives.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.040
- I don't just wanna be a
provider, I wanna lead people.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.570
And I believe at Yale,

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.980
I get the opportunities to learn

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.704
how to be the right type
of leader I wanna become,

00:01:29.704 --> 00:01:30.537
but I wanna do it on a national level.

00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:33.900
Whatever I'm doing in 10
years could be for the CDC,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.370
could be for the White House,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.170
you're gonna see my name
and they're gonna know

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.003
I went to Yale.

00:01:38.003 --> 00:01:41.250
- I think of nursing as
the family profession.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.710
My mother was a nurse, but you know,

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.990
my grandfather was a nurse.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.070
After his little store on the
lower East side of New York

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.560
was wiped out in the depression,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.090
he then developed a profession
of caring for elderly

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.860
and ill individuals.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.790
- My wife's a nurse.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.450
I have a very strong sense of
what it means to be a nurse.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.960
It is an altruistic work of serving people

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.300
in moments when they
are most need of help.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.430
And so when I think of the nursing school,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.193
I think of the service that they render

00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:15.840
to all parts of our community.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.920
During Covid, every one of the
people that gave me a vaccine

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.100
was somebody who was a student
at the Yale Nursing School.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.160
We support the nurses and
the nurses support us,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.710
and that service makes a huge
difference to the communities

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.000
in which we live.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.000
- So Yale's primary purpose
is to improve the world today

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.450
and for future generations.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.450
That is a mission of every
nurse who graduates from Yale.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.260
They do it by leading organizations,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.020
they do it through advanced practice,

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.360
they do it through clinical service,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.880
they do it through nursing education,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.830
they do it through science,

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.000
but they are all focused
on improving the world.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.650
That's what Yale is all about.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.263
That's what the Yale School
of Nursing is all about.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.420
- You come to nursing because
you have this incredible need

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.220
to be part of humanity

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:19.220
and to bring health to all
kinds of different people.

00:03:19.515 --> 00:03:20.348
- The ability to be a nurse,

00:03:20.348 --> 00:03:22.200
be a nurse practitioner, be a mom,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.930
and also inspire the next
generation of nurses.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.100
I mean, that's huge.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:31.100
- We bring dedication
and passion for the work

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.080
alongside of this desire

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.080
to bring change to the system
at large and to open ourselves

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.200
to approaching healthcare
from different perspectives.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.160
- What I see is just such an
exciting view of the future,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.660
of what they want to do, right?

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.010
And how much they want to take on,

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.800
and how much they want to
improve health for all.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.180
This is the call of nursing.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.490
- The ability to impact change,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.310
the ability to conduct
research that is important

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.530
not only to myself, but also to the health

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.850
and wellbeing of others.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.280
- We need to be thinking
about not just illness,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.620
but wellness. And not just wellness,

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.180
but what does it mean for a person

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.290
in the context of their life.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.300
- What we're looking
for in the next century

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.120
is who we are continuing to become.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.000
And that is what makes
this place ever exciting.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.320
- I'm just passionate about
educating the next generation

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.700
of nurses.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.460
We have varieties of opportunities

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.430
to make some transformational impact

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.697
all the way across the world.

00:04:31.697 --> 00:04:33.063
- YSN in one word.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:34.980
- Pioneering.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.080
- Impactful.
- Trailblazing.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.670
- Exciting.
- Empowering.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:39.810
- Inspirational.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.730
- It's leadership.
- The people.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.320
- Unique.
- Community.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.090
- Integrity.
- Resilient.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.010
- Opportunities.
- Innovation.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:51.210
- It's where tradition and
innovation across each other

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.769
and come together to
contribute to the betterment

00:04:54.769 --> 00:04:55.986
of our society.

00:04:55.986 --> 00:04:58.569
(upbeat music)

00:04:59.838 --> 00:05:03.005
(audience applauding)

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.450
- Good afternoon and welcome
to the official kickoff

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.450
of the Yale School of Nursing
Centennial Celebration.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:19.860
I am Melissa Galinato,

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.930
a member of the Centennial
Planning Committee

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.270
and a second year pediatric
nurse practitioner

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.890
primary care student.

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.870
It is an honor to be
the first to welcome you

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.300
to this exciting moment
in the school's history

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.640
and to introduce President Salovey.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.250
On behalf of my nursing
and midwifery classmates,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.260
I like to say what a
remarkable achievement

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.490
it is to be here.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.410
Given the diversity of our backgrounds

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:41.910
and unique experiences,

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.880
I look forward to witnessing
how we collectively contribute

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.980
to the advancement of YSN's mission

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.930
of better health for all people.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.170
When I, along with the
rest of class of 2024,

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.280
graduate in the spring,

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.350
we'll follow nearly 6,000 alumni

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.510
into all aspects of healthcare.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.510
MSN graduates will become clinicians

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:01.470
in every imaginable care setting.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.110
Our doctor of nursing
practice degree recipients

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.500
will lead health systems and
influence healthcare policy.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.910
Those of us who earn a PhD

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.550
will continue to teach the
next generation of providers

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.690
through interprofessional
education, research, and practice.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.790
It is clear YSN alumni have

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.560
and will continue to innovate,

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.780
lead and transform healthcare in ways

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.870
not unimaginable for
nurses a hundred years ago.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:26.670
And now it is my privilege to introduce

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.610
the 23rd president of Yale, Peter Salovey.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.650
President Salovey has both celebrated

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.510
and championed YSN's mission

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.850
and our values in nursing and midwifery.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:36.683
In the summer,

00:06:36.683 --> 00:06:38.220
he attended the disaster simulation

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.630
for our first year GPEN students

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:41.490
and eagerly delved into conversations

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.250
to learn about the students'
intense experiences.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.470
President Salovey firmly
believes in the impact

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:47.850
of our chosen paths,

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.070
so much so that he and his loved ones

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:53.430
endowed his Salovey Family
Nursing Scholarship here at Yale

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.140
or at Yale School of Nursing.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.360
It is a privilege to have
such a dedicated advocate

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.000
for YSN in the top position
at this university,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.730
and we welcome his presence
here today as a testament

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.820
to YSN's integral role
in shaping Yale's future.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.160
I invite you to read the
rest of his formal biography

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.200
in our program and allow him to share more

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.720
about his commitment to supporting YSN.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.307
I would now like to welcome to
the podium President Salovey.

00:07:16.307 --> 00:07:18.743
(audience applauding)

00:07:18.743 --> 00:07:20.660
- Thank you very much.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:26.790
Well, thank you very much
for that kind introduction.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:30.660
I very much appreciate it.
And good afternoon everyone.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.040
So nice to see you all

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:37.040
and all supporting the
school in its hundredth year.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:41.970
It's an enormous pleasure to
be part of the great history

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:44.190
of the Yale School of Nursing,

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:49.190
and I very much have been looking forward

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.710
to this centennial moment.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:56.070
I'm also delighted that with us today,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.740
our Yale School of Nursing faculty,

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:02.550
Yale School of Nursing
staff members, students,

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.040
alumni who are now practitioners,

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.643
other educators, other researchers.

00:08:09.792 --> 00:08:11.100
I'm delighted you all with us today.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.970
And I'm especially delighted
that our new dean has arrived,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:15.810
Azita Emami.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:16.957
And so we give her-

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:21.597
(audience applauding)

00:08:24.363 --> 00:08:26.367
Ann, it's so great to see you as well.

00:08:26.367 --> 00:08:29.130
And that perfect time for a handoff

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.889
is at the hundred year mark, right?

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:32.722
(audience laughing)

00:08:32.722 --> 00:08:33.990
98, not enough.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.050
102, maybe too many a hundred is perfect.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.253
A hundred is perfect.

00:08:40.088 --> 00:08:43.890
I'm also delighted that
Rajiv Shah is with us

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:45.930
from the Rockefeller Foundation.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:48.270
He'll make some remarks in a few moments,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:52.695
but it's very meaningful
for us to have him here

00:08:52.695 --> 00:08:56.790
with us because the Rockefeller Foundation

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.550
was here right from the beginning.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.560
He'll tell you a little bit
more about that history.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.230
But it was gifts from the
Rockefeller Foundation

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:08.610
that essentially allowed
Yale to create this school.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:12.303
Well, 1923, a hundred years
ago, what was happening,

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.120
the foundation, Rockefeller
Foundation's Goldmark report

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:23.120
suggested that a transformation
in nursing education

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.420
was needed.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.360
The word they used to
describe nursing education

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.873
up to that time was inadequate.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.400
Not that's the word they used,
the report wasn't inadequate.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:38.523
And in partnership with
the Rockefeller Foundation,

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:43.920
early nursing leaders
like Dean Annie Goodrich

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.673
set out to make this the solution,

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.410
make Yale the solution
to those inadequacies

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.470
in nursing education.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:57.060
So we became the first school

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.480
to offer a full educational experience

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.040
for nurses in training

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.673
rather than an apprenticeship program.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:12.380
The curriculum that Dean Goodrich
and others pulled together

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.760
talked about future nurses,

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.130
and this is amazing that
this is a hundred years ago,

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:21.960
this language, future nurses
as scientifically informed,

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:26.777
technically expert, and
socially experienced, right?

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:31.200
It still sounds like
exactly what we do today

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.760
in the school of Nursing.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:33.930
And in fact,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.960
there's a whole history
of nursing leadership

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.350
that came from this school.

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.120
I won't go over it with you.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.390
I'll just mention one Dean Florence Wald,

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:48.780
who brought the hospice
movement from England

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:50.460
to the United States.

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.530
I mention it because Branford Hospice

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:57.090
celebrates its 50th
anniversary this same year

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:00.753
that we celebrate our one hundredths.

00:11:05.468 --> 00:11:08.070
When Yale opened admission for students

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:09.360
with a bachelor's degree,

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.940
but no prior background in nursing

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.180
through what is now called GPEN,

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:20.180
the graduate entry pre specialty
program, that was a first.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.690
By 1975, Yale School of Nursing

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:28.380
has 10 specialty programs and tracks

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:32.700
was at the vanguard of
the nurse practitioner

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:37.320
and new nurse midwifery
educational movement

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.170
at the graduate level.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.980
Fast forward a hundred years
forward and nursing is making,

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:47.040
the school of nursing is making
all kinds of contributions

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.030
across the university,
let me pick just one.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:52.050
As many of you know,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:56.850
we have a planetary solutions initiative.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:01.560
The Planetary Health Initiative
within Planetary Solutions

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:06.560
is a touch point for
the school of nursing.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:12.740
How to essentially deal with
the way in which climate change

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:19.443
is affecting human health.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:24.480
When I think about the next
century of the Yale School

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:27.210
of Nursing, I'm optimistic,

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:31.920
I think nursing's centrality
to this university

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.310
and nursing as a profession centrality

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:37.980
to improving the world now

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.480
and for the future is
just all too obvious.

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:44.700
So in the film you heard about my family,

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:49.050
I sometimes say nursing
was the family business

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.360
in our family.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:55.140
My former brother-in-law was
a nurse, you heard about that.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.780
My grandfather's story is interesting.

00:12:57.780 --> 00:12:59.883
After he was wiped out in the depression,

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.970
when my grandfather used to
talk about being wiped out,

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:06.360
he had a little store that was
maybe about 500 square feet

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.468
and it went bankrupt in the depression.

00:13:08.468 --> 00:13:11.449
But he didn't have anything
to begin with really.

00:13:11.449 --> 00:13:12.410
(audience laughing)

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.070
So that was on the lower
East side of New York.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:15.990
And as you heard, to make a living,

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:20.910
he just cared for people and
he had no formal education.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:25.910
But in those days, on the
testimony of your clients,

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:28.330
you could get a license as an LPN

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:32.280
and I always used to joke, I actually,

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.890
I have it, but I used to say

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:38.520
my grandfather was New
York State LPN number two

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:39.831
or something like that.

00:13:39.831 --> 00:13:40.664
(audience laughing)

00:13:40.664 --> 00:13:42.230
He had to have been one of the first men

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.990
to be licensed as an LPN.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:49.050
But he was, and he
talked about his patients

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:52.380
and he talked about what he had learned

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:57.380
kind of in the trenches of
nursing all my childhood.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:01.470
And so my mother who
married into that family,

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.330
and he always had a special relationship,

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.870
'cause my mother as
you heard, was a nurse.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:11.340
She went to what is formerly
called Jewish Hospital

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:12.660
and Medical Center of Brooklyn,

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.520
but everybody calls it Brooklyn Jewish,

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.170
doesn't exist anymore.

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:20.070
And had that old style of RN education

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:21.480
that wasn't university based

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.850
and was more apprentice oriented.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:24.840
But my childhood,

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.920
I was a latchkey child because
we came home from school,

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:33.920
my brother and I, and we
made ourselves a snack

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:37.270
and we waited for our mom to
come home from the hospital

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:40.320
where she was working with a practice

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:41.153
where she was working,

00:14:41.153 --> 00:14:44.730
ultimately she moved
into geriatric nursing

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.520
toward the last maybe 20
years of her profession

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:51.960
and was the director of
nurses at geriatric hospitals

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.630
first in, well, by that
time we'd left New Jersey,

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.580
we were now in Buffalo, New
York, and later in Los Angeles.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.640
The stories that my grandfather told

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:06.660
that my brother-in-law
told, that my mother told,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:11.220
made an impression on
all of us in my family.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:16.000
And helped us, I think
appreciate how rewarding

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:19.050
a profession can be.

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:21.060
And I know our students feel that way,

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.073
but also how complex it is.

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:27.213
And I would say in more recent years,

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:32.533
how in need of improvement
our healthcare system is.

00:15:34.890 --> 00:15:39.890
Well, it was the public health
crisis ushered in by Covid,

00:15:43.487 --> 00:15:47.820
that I think helped every
one of us who is not a nurse,

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:50.640
or a public health
professional like my wife is,

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:54.420
or a physician helped
all of us who are not

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:57.573
in the health professions
truly appreciate,

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:01.170
truly appreciate how hard this work is,

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:02.793
how needed this work is,

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:07.590
and how in short supply this work is.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:09.690
We're still recovering.

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:11.010
We're not out of the pandemic

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.910
and further we're still
recovering from the effect

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:18.090
that the pandemic had on our workforce

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:19.893
in the health professions.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:23.940
And so a hundredth anniversary comes,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:26.310
you don't plan it for a particular time,

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.650
it comes in the hundredth year,

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:33.650
but the university's response
to the hundredth anniversary,

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:38.280
the fact that we are
matching gifts to the school,

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:40.770
the idea that we are celebrating

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:44.070
the centrality of the school,

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:48.240
I think is not an accident
in coming in these years

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:53.240
as we recover as a society
from this global pandemic.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:55.750
While everyone who is here today

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:01.820
has been involved in helping
position in some way,

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:07.400
the Yale School of Nursing at
the center of Yale University

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:10.810
and help reposition the profession

00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:16.530
as at the center of healthcare,

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:20.100
as delivered in this country
and throughout the world.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:23.580
And I thank you for all of that hard work,

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:27.540
and I thank you for letting me be a part

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.030
of both today's celebration,

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:34.470
but this larger mission and
these larger goals as well.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.080
So thank you all very
much for being here today

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.517
and congratulations to the school.

00:17:39.517 --> 00:17:42.767
(audience applauding)

00:17:48.011 --> 00:17:51.600
Thank you. Now I'd like to
introduce our next speaker,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.750
who is the Yales school
of Nursing's new dean,

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:56.880
Dr. Azita Emami.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:01.080
So Azita is an internationally
recognized nursing leader,

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:02.643
a nursing investigator.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:07.560
She's had a career dedicated
to fostering wellness.

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:10.930
She's had two more, than
two decades in leading

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:17.970
research and teaching
in university settings.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:20.190
This is not, what's the expression?

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:21.780
This is not your first rodeo.

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:23.280
This is not your first rodeo.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.350
This is her third deanship.

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:30.350
And we are so fortunate that she can build

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:34.260
on Ann's accomplishments and lead us

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:39.150
into the next century of nursing at Yale,

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:42.660
that she responded when I
called her and asked her,

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:45.000
would you like a third deanship?

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.610
And she ultimately said yes.

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:51.390
Her work has focused quite a
bit on the social determinants

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.280
that affect health and wellness

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.560
as a part-time health psychologist

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.600
that appeals to me greatly.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:00.510
She's a trustee of the Robert
Wood Johnson Foundation.

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:04.890
She's been an advocate for
health equity and accessibility

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:09.690
for quality health care for
all people, and in fact,

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.990
quality healthcare beyond
the nursing profession.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:14.520
I know she is the right leader

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.947
for the school's second century.

00:19:17.947 --> 00:19:20.683
So please join me in
welcoming her to the podium.

00:19:20.683 --> 00:19:23.933
(audience applauding)

00:19:30.235 --> 00:19:31.068
- Thank you very much,

00:19:31.068 --> 00:19:34.320
President Salovey for
those very kind words.

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:35.790
And good afternoon everyone.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:38.283
It's such a pleasure to be with you today.

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:44.390
I would like to begin
by land acknowledgement

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:48.963
to initiate our conversation.

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:55.170
Yale School of Nursing
is situated on the land

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:59.670
of the past and present Quinnipiac people.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:04.470
It is with deep gratitude that
we honor the land, people,

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:08.070
history and traditions
of the indigenous peoples

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:12.540
who stewarded and the land for generations

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.670
and who are still connected to the land,

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:17.280
as well as the responsibility

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:19.920
that comes with sharing the land.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:24.270
We acknowledge the painful
history and with our mission

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:28.260
at Yale School of Nursing of
better health for all people,

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:32.640
we embrace the commitment
to health, justice,

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:35.130
and care for all communities,

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:39.963
especially those who suffer
unacceptable health disparities.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:48.330
So a centennial celebration
honors the past,

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:53.070
but it also is an appropriate occasion

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:58.070
to envision the future that
will be built on that past.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:03.470
I want to take a few moments
today to share with you

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:07.080
the future I see for the school of nursing

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:10.473
and how the past makes
that future possible.

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:15.360
I see a future where nursing leads the way

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:19.710
on promoting wellness rather than focusing

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:22.710
and addressing illnesses.

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:26.550
I see a future where better
health for all people

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:28.053
becomes a reality.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:34.190
My vision of the future
sees the healthcare system

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:39.090
in which everyone feels
comfortable, respected,

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:41.070
welcome and understood,

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:45.753
because we have educated nurses
to create that environment.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:51.800
My vision of the future
sees vastly improved access

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:52.980
to healthcare,

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:57.150
because nurses are
practicing to the full scope

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:00.780
of their capabilities, including delivery,

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:04.980
delivering urgently needed primary care

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:08.043
to historically underserved populations.

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:13.080
And perhaps most importantly,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:17.760
I see a future in which our
advocacy result in addressing

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.610
the many social determinants of health

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:25.610
that are the root cause
of disparities in outcomes

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.743
and the lack of wellness for many people.

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.940
These disparities are not new,

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:36.813
nor will they be easily eliminated.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:40.530
These are deeply entrenched realities

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:44.790
reflecting the structural
and systemic inequities

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:49.790
resulting from racism,
classism, and inequitable access

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:55.083
to the resources people
need to live and thrive.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.980
At Yale School of Nursing,

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:03.690
we are the heirs to a
legacy of innovation,

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:07.350
creativity, and advocacy.

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:11.970
Yale School of Nursing was
founded as a break from the past

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:15.273
as President Salovey shared with you.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:20.970
For the first time, Nursing
was an academic discipline

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:24.693
with its own body of knowledge
to be taught and mastered.

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:30.650
Nurses were no longer just
a source of cheap labor

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:32.850
for the medical wards,

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:36.120
they were now educated to provide care

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.030
that enable people to get better.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:43.263
This was, believe me or not,
a groundbreaking concept.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:48.840
Our first dean, Annie Goodrich,

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:51.783
was the first female dean at Yale.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.740
We were among the first nursing schools

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.900
to grant a master's degree

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:01.623
and to require prior nursing
experience to admission.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:08.340
In the 1970s, we introduced
the idea of graduate entry,

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:12.033
pre specialty nursing GPEN program.

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:15.450
And at that point of time,

00:24:15.450 --> 00:24:18.690
just thinking about making a non-nurse,

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:23.690
an advanced nurse
practitioner in three years,

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.270
it was something that
no one would believe in

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.313
to be accomplished, but we did it.

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:34.710
Our legacy of leadership continues today,

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:36.480
mostly recently with the launch

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:40.443
of the brand new online MSN program.

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:45.990
Yale School of Nursing
began breaking barriers

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:50.760
at its inception and has
never stopped doing so.

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:55.760
We will continue adding
to that admirable legacy

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.590
as we enter our new century.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:03.590
I believe there is a widespread consensus

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.240
that the American healthcare system

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:11.070
is not delivering optimal results.

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:14.973
There's less consensus
about how to make it better.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:18.873
Well, the short answer is nurses.

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:24.360
As one of the leading schools of nursing,

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:28.650
we have an opportunity and an obligation

00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:32.550
to educate the nurses who
will lead in creating change

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:35.793
and delivering better
health for all people.

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:40.140
Yale School of Nursing
was a paradigm shift

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:42.120
at its inception.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:47.120
And we will be that once
again in our second century.

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:53.760
Our nurse graduates will
continue being compassionate,

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:55.860
capable clinicians.

00:25:55.860 --> 00:26:00.860
They will also be powerful,
persuasive advocates,

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.190
inquisitive, accomplished researchers,

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:10.190
inspirational educators, capable
and creative policy makers,

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.940
respected and respectful
community members,

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.160
and above all else,

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:21.720
leaders in redefining what wellness is

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:24.933
and how it can be made
the standard of care.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:31.500
Today's theme is
imagining the next century

00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:34.320
of better health for all people.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:37.113
I want to do more than just imagining it.

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:43.020
I want to make it happen by
leading a school of nursing

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:47.370
that shares a vision of
a future of wellness,

00:26:47.370 --> 00:26:51.510
equitable access to healthcare
and a system of care

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:55.410
that lives up to promises of equity made

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:57.243
at this country's foundation.

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:00.633
Make no mistake,

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:06.540
getting to that goal is
a very challenging task.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.340
But for a century,

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:11.580
Yale School of Nursing has identified

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:13.923
and met challenges in healthcare.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.193
It's who we are and it's what we do.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:21.780
We can thank our
predecessors for the legacy

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:26.780
and we can best honor them
by committing to following

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:32.343
in their footsteps and
looking forward, not away.

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:38.700
We cannot accept as
insurmountable the divisions

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:41.640
and equities of lack of wellness

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.433
that are today's reality in our country.

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:49.833
We cannot accept that healthcare
disparities are inevitable,

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:55.113
and we absolutely cannot
accept that the idea,

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.510
the idea that meeting,

00:27:57.510 --> 00:28:02.010
the meeting and eliminating
these challenges

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:04.383
is too costly or too difficult.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:09.630
Nursing is American's
most trusted profession.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:13.500
Tens of millions of
people are counting on us

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:18.500
to create a new healthcare
system in this country

00:28:18.630 --> 00:28:22.320
to focus on improving
the health of all people.

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:27.320
We are the voice and advocates
for those who have no voices.

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:31.923
We are Yale School of Nursing.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:36.180
We will be the clinicians and advocates

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:39.930
and voices that inspire a transformation

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:41.523
in Americans health.

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:46.050
So thank you very much and
welcome to the next century

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:48.182
of Yale School of Nursing.

00:28:48.182 --> 00:28:51.432
(audience applauding)

00:28:56.741 --> 00:28:58.113
It's now my real pleasure to
introduce our special guest,

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:05.610
Dr. Rajiv Shah is president
of the Rockefeller Foundation,

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:09.510
which made the grant,
as you heard previously,

00:29:09.510 --> 00:29:13.593
that established Yale School
of Nursing centuries ago.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:16.020
The Rockefeller Foundation

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:19.710
believed in advanced
nurse practice for nurses

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:23.910
when the concept didn't even
exist, no one knew about it.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:27.480
So very pioneering, very, very innovative.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.600
And the foundation continues to identify

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.830
and support transformative changes

00:29:34.041 --> 00:29:36.210
across human and planetary health.

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:38.968
Please join me in welcoming Dr. Shah.

00:29:38.968 --> 00:29:42.218
(audience applauding)

00:29:43.496 --> 00:29:44.610
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:46.277
- Thank you so much.

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:49.170
Good afternoon everyone. How are you?

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:50.670
- [Audience] Good.

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:54.000
- Just outta curiosity,
how many students are here?

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:54.833
Oh, good.

00:29:54.833 --> 00:29:57.060
Well, thank you for
being here in particular

00:29:57.060 --> 00:30:00.150
and for taking on the
mission you're taking on.

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:01.290
Thank you President Salovey

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:03.360
for that outstanding set of remarks

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:05.580
and for sharing with
us why you care so much

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:08.610
and have in your family for generations.

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:12.480
And thank you Dean Emami for
inaugurating the new century

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:15.810
for this great school and for
your extraordinary leadership

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:18.720
that I know will help this
school reach even higher heights.

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:21.150
So it's really exciting to be here

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:23.760
and it's great to be with this community.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:27.810
And to honor a little bit
of our shared history.

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:28.800
In many ways,

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:31.860
the Rockefeller Foundation
and the Yale School of Nursing

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.410
were founded for the same reason,

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:38.400
effectively to use science
and the frontiers of science

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:41.220
to improve the health of everyone.

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:42.180
And in fact,

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:46.680
before the foundation was
even incorporated in 1913,

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:50.550
John D. Rockefeller Sr. gave
more than a million dollars

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:53.670
to try to eradicate
hookworm a treatable disease

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:56.670
that affected, remember this number,

00:30:56.670 --> 00:31:00.633
more than 40% of people
living in the American South.

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:03.450
The result of that work,

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.580
which created an
infrastructure at the time

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:08.340
called the General Education Board,

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:12.660
resulted in creating America's
county-based public health

00:31:12.660 --> 00:31:13.653
infrastructure.

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.520
That work also provided the basic protocol

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:18.990
for the Rockefeller Foundation's

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:21.150
global public health campaigns,

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:23.490
which began thereafter
and targeted a number

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:26.103
of important infectious diseases.

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:31.380
The goal of those campaigns
were all about making health

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:34.620
and the access to health more equitable,

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:37.533
in particular for vulnerable communities.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:41.700
Back then, the definition
of vulnerable community

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:42.840
was very large,

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:45.960
because the application
of science in medicine,

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:49.170
in nursing and in public
health was haphazard

00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:50.730
and unorganized.

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:53.940
And while I am not the
author, as you can tell,

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:57.180
of the original report the
president made reference to,

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:01.710
I think the indictment of
medicine and the health sciences

00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:03.480
at the turn of the last century

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:06.210
was that it was not a
science-based discipline.

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:09.210
And the foundation worked with
institutions across the world

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:11.613
to create a science-based discipline.

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:15.810
Within all of the extraordinary inequities

00:32:15.810 --> 00:32:19.140
that existed in health
in the United States

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:21.870
and around the world at that time,

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:24.930
nurses were a bright spot.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.970
We've heard about Annie Goodrich

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:30.240
and the extraordinary history she's had,

00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:32.190
but I am new to her story.

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:34.950
And so it was fun for me to learn

00:32:34.950 --> 00:32:38.370
that she became a nurse before
the turn of the century.

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:40.170
And as nursing superintendent,

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:42.540
when the first World War broke out,

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:46.230
she warned that the US
Army was deeply unprepared

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:50.370
and it would lead to significant
competitive disadvantage.

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:51.270
As a result,

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:53.220
she was chosen to organize and lead

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:56.250
the Army School of Nursing
as its first ever dean,

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:58.920
for which she received the
distinguished service medal,

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:03.750
probably the first nurse
to receive that medal.

00:33:03.750 --> 00:33:05.820
After her service to the Army,

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:09.603
she of course came here and
founded this amazing school.

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:13.830
The Yale School of Nursing
under her leadership

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:18.330
was founded with a
$150,000 five year grant

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:20.012
from the Rockefeller Foundation.

00:33:20.012 --> 00:33:21.150
(audience laughing)

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:22.710
I asked President Salovey,

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:26.460
if he could start a
school today for $150,000.

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:27.744
He said no.

00:33:27.744 --> 00:33:28.860
(audience laughing)

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:30.780
And we did a little math and we learned

00:33:30.780 --> 00:33:33.900
that we think that's
about 2.7 or $2.8 million

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:35.250
in today's money.

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:38.400
And just a few years later,
at the end of that grant,

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:43.080
when the foundation reviewed
the progress of the mission,

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:46.830
which was bringing science
and educational discipline

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:51.750
and lifting up the baseline
education process for nurses

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:54.900
and nursing, they decided
it was so successful,

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:57.990
they awarded another $1 million endowment,

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:00.150
which really allowed
the school to flourish.

00:34:00.150 --> 00:34:03.630
We think that's about $18
million in today's money.

00:34:03.630 --> 00:34:04.950
I also asked the president,

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:07.200
if you could start a school
today for 18 million,

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:08.639
he said, no.

00:34:08.639 --> 00:34:10.290
(audience laughing)

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:13.530
So Yale and the foundation
learned together

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:17.730
that nursing was in fact
the key to public health

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:21.120
in the United States
in that window of time.

00:34:21.120 --> 00:34:22.170
To me, that's a lesson

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:26.220
that I think is extraordinarily
relevant today for,

00:34:26.220 --> 00:34:29.580
I know how many of you as students here

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:32.670
have put your efforts and
energies into global health,

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:34.350
which is where the foundation spends

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:36.990
a lot of its resources today.

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:39.600
And we've seen over the
last 15 or 20 years,

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:41.520
extraordinary progress.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:45.150
Just to give you one example
about the turn of the century,

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:46.500
around 2000,

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:49.650
there were about nine
to 10 million children

00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:52.380
under the age of five who died every year,

00:34:52.380 --> 00:34:55.170
mostly of easily preventable causes

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:57.930
and almost entirely in developing nations.

00:34:57.930 --> 00:34:59.940
That number was effectively cut in half

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:01.830
until about a few years ago

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:03.780
and has been steadily creeping up

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:05.820
each of the last few years.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:08.370
And the truth is, this week is,

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:09.600
we call it Climate Week,

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:13.170
or the Sustainable Development
Goals week in New York

00:35:13.170 --> 00:35:14.430
at the United Nations.

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:16.950
And it's been pretty grim
to look at the numbers

00:35:16.950 --> 00:35:18.630
because over the last few years,

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:21.600
we are systematically unwinding years,

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:24.420
even decades of progress
in fighting hunger,

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:25.740
fighting poverty,

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:28.140
and certainly in our common mission

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:30.150
of standing up for health equity

00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:32.460
and international public health.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:34.140
And so now more than ever,

00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:37.530
we have to recognize that
nurses are, once again,

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:41.430
have always been a
bright spot in the fight

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:43.710
from those of you who
helped vaccinate migrant,

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:47.370
transgender and other vulnerable
people against Covid 19

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:48.203
in India,

00:35:48.203 --> 00:35:50.970
to those who are tracking
maternal and newborn health,

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:53.550
using new digital tools in Uganda,

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:57.240
to those who administered
PCR tests to Afghan refugees

00:35:57.240 --> 00:35:59.190
right here in New Haven.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:00.750
Your point of service,

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:03.600
point of leadership and points of science

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:08.600
are the hopes we have for
changing the status of health

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:11.820
and public health at home
and around the world.

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:15.480
That insight, which was
true many, many years ago,

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:17.160
is still true today.

00:36:17.160 --> 00:36:19.380
And so it's a real honor
to get to be with you

00:36:19.380 --> 00:36:21.270
and celebrate your history.

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:24.060
And it's even more
exciting to get to be here

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:25.620
and participate in a conversation

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:27.660
where we'll talk about your future,

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:29.820
because I believe now more than ever

00:36:29.820 --> 00:36:33.180
with the pace of technology,
the pace of science,

00:36:33.180 --> 00:36:36.090
new tools related to
artificial intelligence

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:40.470
and new realities about a
society slowly but steadily,

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:43.110
opening up to include everybody,

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:44.700
what this school has stood for,

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:47.130
its values, its commitments, its science,

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.830
and its people will be
more transformational

00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:52.559
in this century to come. Thank you.

00:36:52.559 --> 00:36:55.809
(audience applauding)

00:37:11.409 --> 00:37:15.631
- I think that our microphones
are on. Do you hear us well?

00:37:15.631 --> 00:37:16.631
Okay, great.

00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:20.202
So I would like to begin

00:37:20.202 --> 00:37:21.870
by thanking both of you

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:25.530
for your very powerful
inspirational remarks.

00:37:25.530 --> 00:37:28.110
And thank you very much
for being with us today

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:30.690
to discuss very important topic

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:33.540
that I think that's many of us

00:37:33.540 --> 00:37:35.913
are very interested to learn more about.

00:37:37.714 --> 00:37:40.530
So before we start our conversation,

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:43.320
I just would like to share with you that,

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:45.720
after our conversation
there will be opportunities

00:37:45.720 --> 00:37:48.120
for the audience to ask questions.

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:51.840
So while we are carrying
out our conversation,

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:54.540
please start thinking
about what are the topics

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:59.540
that you would like to ask
our special guests here today?

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:05.480
Yeah. I would like to begin
with my first questions

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:07.393
to both of you.

00:38:07.393 --> 00:38:09.270
A hundred years ago,

00:38:09.270 --> 00:38:10.650
the Rockefeller Foundation

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:14.113
founded the Yale School of
Nursing at the first academic,

00:38:14.113 --> 00:38:17.883
as the first academic nursing
school in this country.

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:23.280
We are honored to be here
a hundred years later

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:27.270
talking about the importance
of how this workforce

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:29.523
has evolved and progressed.

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:32.100
So for each of you,

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:37.100
what is the most surprising
change you have witnessed,

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.863
or heard about in the
profession of nursing?

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:43.080
Who would like to start?

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:48.223
- I'm happy to start. I,
you know, I get to see,

00:38:48.223 --> 00:38:51.180
I ran a federal agency called
USAID for President Obama,

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:53.100
US Agency for International Development.

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:56.230
And so I've had a chance to see nursing

00:38:57.812 --> 00:39:01.380
and the practice of nursing
in societies around the world.

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:05.040
And the thing I find is that
the secret transformation

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:09.150
that's happening is if you go to a village

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:10.530
in northern India,

00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:14.130
or a slum outside of Nairobi,

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:18.180
where some of us just were a
few weeks ago called Cabera,

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:23.180
you'll meet nurses who are
taking their smartphones

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:26.790
and an extraordinary amount
of technology with them

00:39:26.790 --> 00:39:29.370
to the interface with patients.

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:33.240
And it is transforming their capacity

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:37.350
to address malnutrition,
to identify disease,

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:41.940
to rapidly determine how
to triage communities

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:43.140
and making them frankly,

00:39:43.140 --> 00:39:45.690
ever more powerful and
ever more successful.

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:48.300
There's an outstanding long piece today

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:50.910
and or yesterday maybe
in the "New York Times"

00:39:50.910 --> 00:39:52.650
about the community health workers,

00:39:52.650 --> 00:39:56.790
which of course are not
anywhere near the high level

00:39:56.790 --> 00:40:01.320
of Yale educated school
of nursing graduates.

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:04.440
But I think there's going
to be a trend over time

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:08.010
with the democratization
of knowledge, information,

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:10.860
and technology at the point of service

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:11.760
and the point of care,

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:14.760
so that over time we'll
finally be able to reach

00:40:14.760 --> 00:40:18.810
absolutely everyone with the
ethic and spirit of nursing

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:20.310
with which the school was founded,

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:23.400
which is the country at the
time needed 50,000 nurses,

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:25.110
had 11,000,

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:28.820
and was asking itself how
could you quickly generate

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:31.170
40,000 additional super skilled,

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:33.690
highly trained professionals.

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:37.110
I think that's the kind
of thinking we need today

00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:39.150
in countries around the world,

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:43.770
if we're to turn around the
reality that we're losing ground

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:45.450
on fighting malaria, deaths,

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:47.820
or dealing with under five mortality,

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:52.820
or have an increase in neonatal
mortality in many settings.

00:40:54.330 --> 00:40:57.450
And even just dealing with
the extraordinary disparities

00:40:57.450 --> 00:40:59.190
in this country that dean,

00:40:59.190 --> 00:41:01.980
you mentioned in your
opening part of your remarks.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:04.647
So that's the thing I'm
both most excited about

00:41:04.647 --> 00:41:07.038
and most surprised by is how pervasive

00:41:07.038 --> 00:41:09.600
that access to technology is going to be

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:11.490
when it really hits.

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:12.750
- Thank you.

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:16.260
- So let me answer that question
in a little different way,

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:19.170
and I don't know if
surprise is the emotion,

00:41:19.170 --> 00:41:21.700
but certainly surprise and thrilled

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:26.883
is the emotion that I feel
when I think about this.

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:29.340
So when I was an undergraduate,

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:32.280
I took a class called medical sociology.

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:35.820
And one of the interesting
aspects of medical sociology

00:41:35.820 --> 00:41:40.050
as taught in the 1970s
was a set of experiments,

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:44.760
field experiments done mostly
in the 50 and early 60s

00:41:44.760 --> 00:41:49.760
on status and the health
professionals, health professions.

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:56.030
And they typically set
up a situation in which

00:41:56.622 --> 00:41:59.470
a nurse would discover
in a hospital setting

00:42:00.540 --> 00:42:05.540
that a physician had ordered
the wrong dose of a medication

00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:10.560
or had, you know, set out
or delivered the wrong dose.

00:42:10.560 --> 00:42:12.210
And in various ways,

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:14.580
there's a whole set of
experiments in various ways,

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:15.930
the question was,

00:42:15.930 --> 00:42:19.500
would the nurse confront
the physician and say,

00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:21.330
you've got the wrong drug here,

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:23.630
you've got the wrong
amount of this drug here.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:27.540
And in the 1950s, again and again,

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:29.970
what was demonstrated
is the nurses all knew

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:34.623
there was a mistake and many
of them didn't say anything.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.070
And this was taught in health,

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:42.330
in the sociology of health
as an example of status

00:42:42.330 --> 00:42:43.740
and status characteristics.

00:42:43.740 --> 00:42:47.310
Some of it was gendered, some
of it was professional status,

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:50.970
and how it could lead
the status consciousness

00:42:50.970 --> 00:42:54.783
could lead to mistakes in healthcare.

00:42:56.460 --> 00:43:01.460
What's wonderful and surprising
isn't the right word,

00:43:02.010 --> 00:43:06.570
but thrilling is you couldn't
replicate those studies today.

00:43:06.570 --> 00:43:09.150
You couldn't get that result today,

00:43:09.150 --> 00:43:12.540
the way in which nurses are
incorporated as equal members

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:16.080
of a team, respected as a profession,

00:43:16.080 --> 00:43:19.920
and are expected to say, sorry, doc,

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:22.050
you don't have that right.

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:24.570
I saw it myself when I
was a psychology intern

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:26.640
at the West Haven VA Hospital

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:29.400
just a couple of decades later.

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:33.960
And that says a lot about the profession,

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:36.270
it says a lot about education,

00:43:36.270 --> 00:43:37.770
it says a lot about the kinds of people

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:39.540
attracted to nursing.

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:43.510
It says a lot about gender equality

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:48.813
and I think it's a, of course,

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:54.420
results in the delivery of
safer and better healthcare

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:55.253
for everyone.

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:57.903
- Great answers, thank you.

00:43:58.890 --> 00:44:02.370
The next question is
to all of us actually,

00:44:02.370 --> 00:44:04.909
and I would like to answer it first.

00:44:04.909 --> 00:44:06.270
(audience laughing)

00:44:06.270 --> 00:44:07.140
- Good idea.

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:10.824
- Following your delighted surprise.

00:44:10.824 --> 00:44:12.750
(all laughing)

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:17.750
So what is the one baseline
improvement you see as critical

00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:22.170
to better health in the United States

00:44:22.170 --> 00:44:24.600
and then internationally?

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:28.410
And I have been thinking
about it for a long time,

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:32.670
and I would like to say that
the most critical baseline,

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:34.320
based on my opinion,

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:38.810
is a universal healthcare
access to everyone

00:44:38.810 --> 00:44:42.089
through affordable care

00:44:42.089 --> 00:44:46.050
and with a very sharp focus on prevention,

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:49.770
health promotion that will be done

00:44:49.770 --> 00:44:52.350
through very strong primary care

00:44:52.350 --> 00:44:55.470
and of course delivered by nurses.

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:56.610
So what about you?

00:44:56.610 --> 00:44:59.070
- Yeah, you know,, I would
answer in a very similar way.

00:44:59.070 --> 00:45:03.030
There is no doubt that the
economics of healthcare

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:04.680
would be improved dramatically

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:06.993
if we put more emphasis on prevention.

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:10.410
And you know, as a psychologist,

00:45:10.410 --> 00:45:13.380
I know that as a nurse professional,

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:15.010
many of you know that

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:19.140
the most expensive things to thing to do

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:21.210
is to fix problems after they happen

00:45:21.210 --> 00:45:22.197
rather than prevent them.

00:45:22.197 --> 00:45:26.760
And so the emphasis on
behavior, and lifestyle factors,

00:45:26.760 --> 00:45:28.500
and environmental factors,

00:45:28.500 --> 00:45:31.480
and the prevention of
illness before it happens

00:45:32.520 --> 00:45:35.170
is partly what's going to, I think save

00:45:36.496 --> 00:45:40.920
our healthcare system in this country.

00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:42.060
So I, you know,

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:46.283
I think we have to really reorient around

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:49.680
a prevention model rather than

00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:54.573
a model focused on repair.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:58.920
- Yeah, I'll agree with that,
but use maybe different words.

00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:03.920
I think wellness is the,
and the science of wellness

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:08.850
and achieving wellness
will ultimately be the path

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:12.120
to help address large scale improvements

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:14.880
in population health,
particularly in the United States.

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:16.470
Eileen O'Connor is with me,

00:46:16.470 --> 00:46:20.490
she's a former Yale
administrator and leader here.

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:23.760
So we were excited to come here together,

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:26.880
but during Covid we
worked and through Eileen,

00:46:26.880 --> 00:46:29.340
helped, worked with the
Yale School of Nursing

00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:33.243
and with Yale University on
testing access in our country,

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:35.760
and you might not remember this,

00:46:35.760 --> 00:46:40.290
but in March and April of 2020, you know,

00:46:40.290 --> 00:46:43.920
actually testing was
not commonly available.

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:46.800
The United States had
rejected through the CDC,

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:51.800
a South Korean testing format
and our own format was faulty.

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:55.470
And the government at the highest level

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:56.880
of our federal government

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.490
was actually against testing, right?

00:46:59.490 --> 00:47:02.670
And so, you know, that's a good example

00:47:02.670 --> 00:47:04.800
of if you sat and asked yourself,

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:08.520
how would you get hundreds
of millions of tests

00:47:08.520 --> 00:47:11.370
manufactured, give people
the confidence to use them

00:47:11.370 --> 00:47:15.390
just to track their own
health and wellness.

00:47:15.390 --> 00:47:17.130
That's the kind of question

00:47:17.130 --> 00:47:19.260
that I think we're gonna be
dealing with over and over.

00:47:19.260 --> 00:47:22.170
Today at the foundation
when we look to the future,

00:47:22.170 --> 00:47:24.390
really coming outta the Covid experience,

00:47:24.390 --> 00:47:27.600
where America had the
highest excess mortality

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:30.600
of any nation on the planet in Covid,

00:47:30.600 --> 00:47:34.200
which is absurd in a country that puts,

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:36.630
as the president made brief reference

00:47:36.630 --> 00:47:40.740
to more than $4 trillion a
year in our healthcare economy,

00:47:40.740 --> 00:47:43.860
we think sustained wellness,

00:47:43.860 --> 00:47:46.410
the reduction of large
scale chronic disease

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:49.380
that made Americans so
vulnerable to mortality

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:51.870
is going to be the only viable answer.

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:56.640
And it'll be you in this room
that develop the science,

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:59.310
but also the way,

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:01.650
the pathway so that
people can listen here,

00:48:01.650 --> 00:48:05.220
engage and participate in
keeping themselves healthy,

00:48:05.220 --> 00:48:09.210
so they're not as vulnerable
as we've been in the past.

00:48:09.210 --> 00:48:10.650
- Thank you.

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:14.670
Dr. Shah, noting that the
foundation has invested heavily

00:48:14.670 --> 00:48:17.430
in solutions related to climate change

00:48:17.430 --> 00:48:19.560
under your leadership,

00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:22.110
what do you see as the
connection between climate

00:48:22.110 --> 00:48:23.280
and human health?

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:26.580
And could you give us some success stories

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:29.670
that you feel the
foundation, or your partners,

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:32.220
or other organizations have had related

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:34.320
to climate and health?

00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:38.010
- Well first I heard the president,

00:48:38.010 --> 00:48:40.890
your reference to planetary health,

00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:43.170
and I'm really glad to hear
that the School of Nursing

00:48:43.170 --> 00:48:45.570
is actively engaged in, you know,

00:48:45.570 --> 00:48:48.270
understanding the correlation

00:48:48.270 --> 00:48:50.220
between planetary health boundaries

00:48:50.220 --> 00:48:51.870
and human health boundaries.

00:48:51.870 --> 00:48:54.960
I think if you look at climate change,

00:48:54.960 --> 00:48:58.350
it's gonna have a few major dislocations

00:48:58.350 --> 00:49:00.570
for large scale population health.

00:49:00.570 --> 00:49:01.980
The first is we just came out

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:04.680
of a once in a century pandemic.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:06.630
You can expect those much more frequently

00:49:06.630 --> 00:49:11.100
because the pressures
that expose populations

00:49:11.100 --> 00:49:13.860
to pandemic threats like zoonotic pressure

00:49:13.860 --> 00:49:16.833
will be much, much higher
as the climate changes.

00:49:17.883 --> 00:49:18.930
A second is,

00:49:18.930 --> 00:49:22.140
we know we're gonna have
much more infectious disease

00:49:22.140 --> 00:49:24.390
in many more places than we do today.

00:49:24.390 --> 00:49:26.130
That's already very obvious.

00:49:26.130 --> 00:49:29.670
So the 50 year history we've
had of a relative decline

00:49:29.670 --> 00:49:31.500
in infectious disease mortality,

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:34.950
morbidity is gonna have
sustained upward pressure now

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:38.520
because of climate over
the next bit of time.

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:40.267
A third is heat and heat,

00:49:40.267 --> 00:49:43.980
I mean, I was with these women
that are salt flat workers

00:49:43.980 --> 00:49:48.423
in the desert in northern
Gujarat in India,

00:49:49.527 --> 00:49:53.220
and they're working in 40
degrees centigrade heat.

00:49:53.220 --> 00:49:56.310
And it's deadly to do that.

00:49:56.310 --> 00:49:59.520
They're already poorly nourished

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:02.160
and they're already the most vulnerable.

00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:05.370
And so the combination
of heat and vulnerability

00:50:05.370 --> 00:50:08.730
will yield extraordinary consequences.

00:50:08.730 --> 00:50:11.520
And the final one that I don't
think most people realize is,

00:50:11.520 --> 00:50:14.400
there's gonna be a 30% reduction

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:18.630
in agricultural productivity
caused by a changing climate.

00:50:18.630 --> 00:50:22.980
And you know, if you look
at just the path for hunger

00:50:22.980 --> 00:50:26.370
and the underlying hidden
hunger or malnutrition

00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:29.310
and micronutrient malnutrition
that you all are experts on,

00:50:29.310 --> 00:50:31.023
and most people don't know about,

00:50:31.920 --> 00:50:34.290
it's gonna make people
much more vulnerable

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:35.610
to all of this in the future.

00:50:35.610 --> 00:50:38.460
So I think there are some big threats.

00:50:38.460 --> 00:50:40.920
The good news is you're on top of it.

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:43.170
We'll count on your
science and your insights

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:46.050
and your ability to get
people to behave differently,

00:50:46.050 --> 00:50:48.840
to keep themselves protected,

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:51.330
but that's why I really believe
your work is more needed

00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:53.490
for the next century than the past one.

00:50:53.490 --> 00:50:55.920
- I don't think I can add to that answer.

00:50:55.920 --> 00:50:58.410
That was just a fabulous answer,

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:02.220
other than to say it is very fortunate

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:05.040
that Yale has a school of nursing,

00:51:05.040 --> 00:51:08.280
a school of public health,
a school of medicine,

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:10.680
a school of the environment,

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:12.450
departments in arts and sciences,

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:14.670
and in engineering and applied sciences

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:17.370
concerned with the environment,

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:22.370
concerned with climate change
and beyond all of that,

00:51:24.030 --> 00:51:27.510
because it is going to take
a multidisciplinary approach

00:51:27.510 --> 00:51:29.460
to solve these problems.

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:34.460
Yes, we all have our particular
emphasis and expertise,

00:51:34.620 --> 00:51:37.650
but it's going to be
working across boundaries,

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:40.743
whether those boundaries
are international,

00:51:41.820 --> 00:51:46.820
or disciplinary to address these problems.

00:51:46.890 --> 00:51:51.423
Sometimes I think as a longtime academic,

00:51:52.410 --> 00:51:55.740
that the international boundaries
may be easier to transcend

00:51:55.740 --> 00:51:59.790
than the disciplinary ones,
and that would be too bad.

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:02.020
And I think we're trying to model

00:52:03.567 --> 00:52:06.600
a more unified approach
here at Yale to addressing,

00:52:06.600 --> 00:52:10.503
I think, the most profound
problem of our lifetime.

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:15.270
- So I would like to follow
on what you just shared,

00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:18.600
because I know that during your tenure,

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:20.910
Yale has invested campus-wide

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:25.650
in climate issues through the
Planetary Solution Project.

00:52:25.650 --> 00:52:29.040
And could you more specifically comment

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:30.630
what role nursing could,

00:52:30.630 --> 00:52:33.600
because I know that the interdisciplinary,

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:35.730
you know, collaboration and the challenge

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:37.980
that you just alluded to,

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:42.630
so, and we have a very, you know,

00:52:42.630 --> 00:52:45.420
strong platform in the school of nursing

00:52:45.420 --> 00:52:46.650
for planetary health.

00:52:46.650 --> 00:52:48.300
I would like to hear your thoughts

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:52.050
about how nursing could
play an important role,

00:52:52.050 --> 00:52:53.310
in your vision.

00:52:53.310 --> 00:52:56.110
- Yeah, I think there's many
roles that nursing can play

00:52:57.180 --> 00:53:02.180
in addressing climate
solutions, planetary solutions.

00:53:03.570 --> 00:53:05.013
Lemme just give a couple.

00:53:06.990 --> 00:53:09.240
First of all,

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:12.840
there is such a strong
emphasis in this nursing school

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:16.260
and at Yale on global health.

00:53:16.260 --> 00:53:19.650
You are already all over the world.

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:23.730
You are already collaborating
with investigators,

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:28.110
and clinicians, and
educators all over the world.

00:53:28.110 --> 00:53:30.150
And you do that in ways that transcend

00:53:30.150 --> 00:53:33.450
some of the political barriers.

00:53:33.450 --> 00:53:34.770
So for example,

00:53:34.770 --> 00:53:38.970
Yale School of Nursing has
worked in China for a long time.

00:53:38.970 --> 00:53:43.970
Politically, we are at a tense moment

00:53:44.310 --> 00:53:45.993
between the two countries.

00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:50.073
In reality,

00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:54.030
climate change is not
addressed without China

00:53:54.030 --> 00:53:56.283
and the US working together just can't,

00:53:57.420 --> 00:54:01.710
these two enormous economies
have to collaborate.

00:54:01.710 --> 00:54:05.433
That's really hard to do right
now at a political level.

00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:10.320
But nurses in China and nurses at Yale

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:12.600
can work together on projects.

00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:15.294
And you know, when I'm in Washington,

00:54:15.294 --> 00:54:17.910
I visit Capitol Hill
and I talk to senators

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:21.480
and members of the house
usually who have a Yale degree,

00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:25.320
but they're on both sides
of the aisle, right?

00:54:25.320 --> 00:54:27.360
And I say, I bring up China,

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:28.830
I'm just using China as an example.

00:54:28.830 --> 00:54:30.330
I bring up China,

00:54:30.330 --> 00:54:33.990
most of the time you get
the current view, right,

00:54:33.990 --> 00:54:35.760
which is quite negative.

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:38.190
And you get it from
both sides of the aisle.

00:54:38.190 --> 00:54:39.023
And then I say,

00:54:39.023 --> 00:54:41.550
do you know we have dual
degree programs in China?

00:54:41.550 --> 00:54:43.650
We have nurses working in China,

00:54:43.650 --> 00:54:46.890
collaborating on capacity
building projects

00:54:46.890 --> 00:54:48.840
and educational projects.

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:52.200
We have a center in Beijing
for the university as a whole,

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:54.900
run by the school of management,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:56.070
We have joint degree programs.

00:54:56.070 --> 00:55:00.570
I say, do you think we should
pull out of all of that?

00:55:00.570 --> 00:55:05.570
Every one of them on both
sides says, no, no, no, no.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:07.740
Stay involved, please stay involved.

00:55:09.030 --> 00:55:11.500
And I think that's because at some level

00:55:13.800 --> 00:55:17.880
they recognize that we have
to look toward the future.

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:20.340
We have huge global problems to solve

00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:22.650
and we're not gonna
solve 'em in isolation.

00:55:22.650 --> 00:55:25.290
So the nurses already doing that.

00:55:25.290 --> 00:55:29.152
Yale School of Nursing
is already doing it.

00:55:29.152 --> 00:55:32.280
And I guess the other, I'll
just mention one other way.

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:34.270
You are seeing

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:41.280
signs, and symptoms, and patient behavior

00:55:41.280 --> 00:55:46.170
often before we know what it
is that we're observing, right?

00:55:46.170 --> 00:55:49.500
The way in which nurses
are trained to observe

00:55:49.500 --> 00:55:51.690
and to observe whole humans,

00:55:51.690 --> 00:55:54.990
and to observe behavior and to observe,

00:55:54.990 --> 00:55:58.890
the kinds of measurable signs and symptoms

00:55:58.890 --> 00:56:01.980
that we use in healthcare

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:06.810
means that those powers of observation,

00:56:06.810 --> 00:56:11.810
that training when put to
use is going to sound alarms

00:56:12.930 --> 00:56:17.610
often before we know what's happening.

00:56:17.610 --> 00:56:18.963
We know what it is.

00:56:20.259 --> 00:56:23.130
And that's kind of a,
what would you call it?

00:56:23.130 --> 00:56:26.073
Sentinel function, maybe
a sentinel function.

00:56:26.910 --> 00:56:30.063
So bring up sentinel
functions on our centennial.

00:56:30.966 --> 00:56:32.629
(audience laughing)

00:56:32.629 --> 00:56:35.070
Try to keep that straight and, you know,

00:56:35.070 --> 00:56:38.670
but it's a role that
primary care providers

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:40.440
are always going to play.

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:44.970
You will see it even before as a society,

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:47.520
we know what it is.

00:56:47.520 --> 00:56:49.320
And that's going to be
very, very important

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:51.420
as we confront new diseases,

00:56:51.420 --> 00:56:55.230
or old diseases appearing in new places

00:56:55.230 --> 00:56:57.450
as the climate changes.

00:56:57.450 --> 00:56:58.283
- Thank you.

00:56:59.193 --> 00:57:04.193
So as both of you very eloquently remarked

00:57:04.200 --> 00:57:09.200
in your speeches about how
the Covid 19 global pandemic

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:13.830
show the world, the heroic, you know,

00:57:13.830 --> 00:57:17.910
impact of nurses and what nurses do,

00:57:17.910 --> 00:57:22.910
it was really wonderful for all us nurses

00:57:23.040 --> 00:57:24.840
to have that recognition.

00:57:24.840 --> 00:57:27.690
But now we are facing a backlash

00:57:27.690 --> 00:57:31.050
in not only United States, but globally.

00:57:31.050 --> 00:57:33.360
There is a huge shortage of nurses.

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:36.930
And I think that part of
is due to burnout because,

00:57:36.930 --> 00:57:40.200
you know, in a couple of
years, the work really,

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:42.780
the nursing work was really devastating,

00:57:42.780 --> 00:57:47.780
and so what do you think
we could do to address now

00:57:49.110 --> 00:57:51.780
the nursing shortage crisis?

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:55.860
What are the ideas that
you might have for?

00:57:55.860 --> 00:57:58.653
- Well, let me mention two, just two.

00:58:00.420 --> 00:58:05.420
Nurses who are educated
as leaders, you know,

00:58:07.890 --> 00:58:10.683
for the nursing profession,
like here at Yale,

00:58:11.850 --> 00:58:13.740
have typically gone to college

00:58:13.740 --> 00:58:17.280
and then have gone to graduate school,

00:58:17.280 --> 00:58:19.173
and they amass a lot of debt.

00:58:20.377 --> 00:58:25.320
At Yale we've been very much
trying over the last decade

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:27.873
to increase access to an education.

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:31.500
And at this university, the highest,

00:58:31.500 --> 00:58:34.530
the students with the
highest debt on graduation,

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:37.320
it's not all from Yale, that's for sure.

00:58:37.320 --> 00:58:40.170
But the students with the
highest debt on graduation

00:58:40.170 --> 00:58:41.850
are our nursing students.

00:58:41.850 --> 00:58:45.360
- Wow.
- That has to end.

00:58:45.360 --> 00:58:46.710
That has to end.

00:58:46.710 --> 00:58:50.187
If we want to see more people
go into the profession.

00:58:50.187 --> 00:58:53.437
(audience applauding)

00:58:57.715 --> 00:58:58.770
I don't come from a wealthy family,

00:58:58.770 --> 00:59:01.110
as you probably figured out from the video

00:59:01.110 --> 00:59:03.510
and from my comments about my family.

00:59:03.510 --> 00:59:05.460
But it is why we did a scholarship here

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:10.460
in the school of nursing
as the first endowment,

00:59:11.236 --> 00:59:15.240
I hope the first among
others that we can do.

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:17.940
And it was really my parents who,

00:59:17.940 --> 00:59:19.890
when I was inaugurated as president,

00:59:19.890 --> 00:59:22.770
my mother called and she said,

00:59:22.770 --> 00:59:26.742
we wanna give you a gift for
becoming president of Yale.

00:59:26.742 --> 00:59:28.320
What do you need?

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:30.018
Need a new camera.

00:59:30.018 --> 00:59:31.800
(audience laughing)

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:35.097
They got me a camera when
I graduated from college.

00:59:35.097 --> 00:59:36.450
(audience laughing)

00:59:36.450 --> 00:59:38.400
And I said, I don't need anything.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:40.620
And I said, well, we wanna get you a gift.

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:44.190
I said, well figure out
something you could do for Yale

00:59:44.190 --> 00:59:46.590
and do it for Yale at the
level that you can do it.

00:59:46.590 --> 00:59:49.500
My dad was a chemistry professor,

00:59:49.500 --> 00:59:52.080
my mother was a nurse, as you heard.

00:59:52.080 --> 00:59:54.825
I said, figure out a
level that you can do it.

00:59:54.825 --> 00:59:55.980
And they came up with the idea,

00:59:55.980 --> 00:59:58.170
we're gonna do a scholarship at Yale

00:59:58.170 --> 01:00:01.062
and we're gonna do it
in the nursing school,

01:00:01.062 --> 01:00:02.790
'cause we know nurses have a lot of debt

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:06.510
and because mom's a nurse
and that's what they did.

01:00:06.510 --> 01:00:08.070
And then we've tried to add more to it

01:00:08.070 --> 01:00:12.780
and friends have added
more to it, and the like.

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:15.161
But I think if that,
we don't have to tell,

01:00:15.161 --> 01:00:18.411
(audience applauding)

01:00:19.633 --> 01:00:22.248
I don't tell that story for your applause.

01:00:22.248 --> 01:00:23.580
I tell that story because it's a story

01:00:23.580 --> 01:00:25.533
that if we could tell more broadly,

01:00:26.550 --> 01:00:28.380
others would follow, others would do,

01:00:28.380 --> 01:00:31.620
they would do their own version of this.

01:00:31.620 --> 01:00:34.420
They just don't know. They
don't know how high the debt,

01:00:35.490 --> 01:00:37.770
as I said, I was gonna talk
about two things we need to do,

01:00:37.770 --> 01:00:40.263
but I talked long enough,
so let me yield to.

01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:44.790
- I don't have anything
to add beyond that,

01:00:44.790 --> 01:00:47.070
except that there should be, you know,

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:49.110
I've always wondered why,

01:00:49.110 --> 01:00:52.710
and I think this is a
higher education question,

01:00:52.710 --> 01:00:55.710
when there's so much sustained demand

01:00:55.710 --> 01:01:00.030
for something that's so
critical to society here

01:01:00.030 --> 01:01:04.170
and around the world, why
can't there be more supply?

01:01:04.170 --> 01:01:05.940
So, you know,

01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:08.670
I don't know whether that means
bigger schools of nursing,

01:01:08.670 --> 01:01:10.290
or more schools of nursing,

01:01:10.290 --> 01:01:14.070
certainly in global health
and on a global basis,

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:17.910
we desperately need many,
many, many more institutions

01:01:17.910 --> 01:01:22.230
that bring what Yale
brings to nursing here

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:23.493
all over the planet.

01:01:24.360 --> 01:01:25.230
And you know,

01:01:25.230 --> 01:01:29.340
any estimate that you look at
in terms of where people will,

01:01:29.340 --> 01:01:32.970
where the needs will be
for society over time,

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:35.490
there's a much greater
share of human labor

01:01:35.490 --> 01:01:37.530
that will go into the caring economy

01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:40.620
for those that look at
it that way in the future

01:01:40.620 --> 01:01:41.880
than in the past

01:01:41.880 --> 01:01:45.060
and in a professionalized
compensated manner

01:01:45.060 --> 01:01:47.070
as opposed to in the informal economy.

01:01:47.070 --> 01:01:50.370
So I just think there's
a supply demand mismatch,

01:01:50.370 --> 01:01:53.010
and I don't have enough
expertise to know why,

01:01:53.010 --> 01:01:54.990
there aren't so many
more schools of nursing.

01:01:54.990 --> 01:01:57.240
Unfortunately, the Rockefeller Foundation,

01:01:57.240 --> 01:02:00.490
if we could start schools
for $150,000, we would do it,

01:02:00.490 --> 01:02:01.323
(audience laughing)

01:02:01.323 --> 01:02:03.603
today we do it all over
the planet, I promise,

01:02:04.830 --> 01:02:06.360
but we can't.

01:02:06.360 --> 01:02:08.073
- And I'll say one more thing.

01:02:09.814 --> 01:02:14.814
When it was February and
certainly March, April, May, 2020,

01:02:16.710 --> 01:02:17.823
what happened?

01:02:19.440 --> 01:02:23.490
This virus that we didn't know much about

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:26.790
starting to move across the
planet, arrives in the US.

01:02:26.790 --> 01:02:30.780
I remember when the first
community transmitted case

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:34.140
in Connecticut arrived when

01:02:34.140 --> 01:02:36.000
our students went home for spring break

01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:37.923
and we told them stay there.

01:02:40.410 --> 01:02:45.000
Nurses went to work,
nurses just went to work.

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:46.623
And they, you know,

01:02:47.534 --> 01:02:50.070
we were trying to keep everybody
else out of the hospital.

01:02:50.070 --> 01:02:53.850
Nurses went into the hospital
and into the clinics.

01:02:53.850 --> 01:02:58.850
And I get a little emotional about this,

01:02:59.430 --> 01:03:03.720
when I saw those signs at
Yale New Haven Hospital,

01:03:03.720 --> 01:03:06.843
for example, that said heroes work here,

01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:10.950
it was an amazing recognition

01:03:10.950 --> 01:03:14.430
of what it meant to be
doing that kind of work

01:03:14.430 --> 01:03:18.180
day in and day out during this pandemic,

01:03:18.180 --> 01:03:20.550
particularly in those early months

01:03:20.550 --> 01:03:24.210
when we didn't know it
was gonna kill everybody

01:03:24.210 --> 01:03:25.680
who it touched.

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:29.640
It was certainly killing
people, people we knew.

01:03:29.640 --> 01:03:31.440
And so, you know,

01:03:31.440 --> 01:03:36.030
that heroes work here signs,

01:03:36.030 --> 01:03:37.200
just made me think,

01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:42.200
how can we make sure that our children,

01:03:43.500 --> 01:03:48.500
that our society recognize
what this work is like

01:03:49.230 --> 01:03:52.080
day in and day out.

01:03:52.080 --> 01:03:53.250
Because if we do that,

01:03:53.250 --> 01:03:57.483
not only will it be more
attractive to kids to go into,

01:03:58.320 --> 01:04:01.680
but it will, but burnout
will be lowered, right?

01:04:01.680 --> 01:04:04.560
People, there's lots of
things that cause burnout

01:04:04.560 --> 01:04:07.770
and sometimes it's some very
bread and butter things,

01:04:07.770 --> 01:04:10.380
but sometimes it's just,

01:04:10.380 --> 01:04:15.300
I wish people knew what it
is like to walk on my shoes.

01:04:15.300 --> 01:04:19.014
And people should know what
it was like to be a nurse

01:04:19.014 --> 01:04:21.030
in the early days of the pandemic.

01:04:21.030 --> 01:04:23.167
- Yes.
- It was to be a hero.

01:04:23.167 --> 01:04:25.080
Yet no nurse thought of
themselves as a hero.

01:04:25.080 --> 01:04:27.480
They just thought they were going to work.

01:04:27.480 --> 01:04:29.628
And it was very impressive.

01:04:29.628 --> 01:04:32.878
(audience applauding)

01:04:34.620 --> 01:04:38.700
- And we know that the number
of lost lives among nurses

01:04:38.700 --> 01:04:42.960
was the highest among all
the frontline, you know,

01:04:42.960 --> 01:04:45.810
providers during the pandemic.

01:04:45.810 --> 01:04:50.020
And despite your excitement,
President Salovey

01:04:50.020 --> 01:04:55.020
about the elimination of,
you know, hierarchical,

01:04:57.900 --> 01:05:00.900
and, you know, structure
in the healthcare services

01:05:00.900 --> 01:05:04.830
that nurses nowadays really,
you know, can speak up

01:05:04.830 --> 01:05:09.830
and if they see malpractice or whatever,

01:05:09.930 --> 01:05:12.490
that is not working in the healthcare

01:05:13.560 --> 01:05:16.080
delivery by physicians.

01:05:16.080 --> 01:05:19.503
But we know that we have
still a long way to go.

01:05:21.157 --> 01:05:25.200
And so I wonder your thoughts about

01:05:27.979 --> 01:05:31.783
the silly, you know, disparities
of power, pay, you know,

01:05:33.720 --> 01:05:35.310
especially in other countries,

01:05:35.310 --> 01:05:40.310
I think that we have gone a
long way in the United States,

01:05:40.470 --> 01:05:45.000
but generally globally nurses
are usually the lowest paid,

01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:48.630
you know, healthcare
professionals and also, you know,

01:05:48.630 --> 01:05:53.630
even in the United States
not being able to practice

01:05:53.940 --> 01:05:57.450
to the full scope of nurses' capability,

01:05:57.450 --> 01:06:02.450
this is a huge even
economic waste of resources.

01:06:03.270 --> 01:06:07.320
So what are your thoughts about
how we can really, you know,

01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:12.320
change the public, you know, image

01:06:12.420 --> 01:06:17.420
of what nurses do and why we
need to really empower nurses

01:06:19.020 --> 01:06:24.020
to practice to the highest
scope of their capabilities

01:06:28.020 --> 01:06:31.224
and get paid for it appropriately?

01:06:31.224 --> 01:06:32.880
(audience laughing)

01:06:32.880 --> 01:06:34.980
- Well, I am pleased to
see that this is changing

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:37.530
and I'm not a economist,

01:06:37.530 --> 01:06:42.450
or sociologist who can
really answer that questions.

01:06:42.450 --> 01:06:43.533
But I will say,

01:06:45.270 --> 01:06:50.270
no movement for change has ever happened,

01:06:51.330 --> 01:06:53.280
I think without allies.

01:06:53.280 --> 01:06:56.580
And I think the allies
are gonna have to be

01:06:56.580 --> 01:06:58.620
other healthcare providers

01:06:58.620 --> 01:07:02.810
and in particular the ally in recognizing

01:07:03.750 --> 01:07:07.180
that nurses who can practice
to their full capability

01:07:09.930 --> 01:07:13.080
or the ally that's gonna
have to recognize that

01:07:13.080 --> 01:07:16.590
the potential ally who's
gonna have to recognize that

01:07:16.590 --> 01:07:20.130
to allow it to happen
is organized medicine.

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:25.130
And it's not a pretty
picture historically.

01:07:25.259 --> 01:07:26.730
(audience laughing)

01:07:26.730 --> 01:07:27.853
And I, you know,

01:07:27.853 --> 01:07:30.887
I don't wanna pick on
anybody or any profession.

01:07:30.887 --> 01:07:32.490
(audience laughing)

01:07:32.490 --> 01:07:33.450
My mother were alive,

01:07:33.450 --> 01:07:36.077
she still would want me to be a doctor.

01:07:36.077 --> 01:07:37.879
(audience laughing)

01:07:37.879 --> 01:07:41.850
But we can, and there's plenty

01:07:41.850 --> 01:07:46.020
of physicians with a
public health orientation,

01:07:46.020 --> 01:07:47.700
with a collaborative orientation

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:51.213
who are very much champions of nursing.

01:07:52.170 --> 01:07:54.360
But we need more of them to be,

01:07:54.360 --> 01:07:56.490
and we need the formal organizations

01:07:56.490 --> 01:08:00.030
that represent physicians to be,

01:08:00.030 --> 01:08:03.210
because I think both of you
have said in your remarks,

01:08:03.210 --> 01:08:04.560
this is part of the solution.

01:08:04.560 --> 01:08:06.600
Nurses are gonna be part of the solution.

01:08:06.600 --> 01:08:09.276
That's true by the way, in my field,

01:08:09.276 --> 01:08:11.628
in the mental health field as well,

01:08:11.628 --> 01:08:14.490
in the field of psychology
and psychiatry as well.

01:08:14.490 --> 01:08:15.480
- Thank you very much.

01:08:15.480 --> 01:08:19.350
I think that now we have
few minutes for questions

01:08:19.350 --> 01:08:20.400
from the audience.

01:08:20.400 --> 01:08:24.073
And could you please in
order for you to be heard

01:08:24.073 --> 01:08:29.073
by everyone, wait for the
microphone to be delivered to you?

01:08:30.120 --> 01:08:31.800
- [Audience Member] Yeah, I
have to find the microphone.

01:08:31.800 --> 01:08:33.112
- Oh, okay.

01:08:33.112 --> 01:08:34.830
(audience laughing)
- It's the one on the podium.

01:08:34.830 --> 01:08:35.823
- Oh, under here.

01:08:37.170 --> 01:08:38.003
Yeah.

01:08:46.380 --> 01:08:50.673
- Thank you. This has been
incredible journey for all of us.

01:08:51.750 --> 01:08:55.650
I love the notion of
primary healthcare, disease,

01:08:55.650 --> 01:08:59.250
you know, primary healthcare,
disease prevention.

01:08:59.250 --> 01:09:02.340
And I think that, and just looking,

01:09:02.340 --> 01:09:05.910
it's not just about promoting
health within individuals,

01:09:05.910 --> 01:09:08.190
or communities that's necessary,

01:09:08.190 --> 01:09:11.130
but structural changes
that need to happen.

01:09:11.130 --> 01:09:15.030
And you know, the concept
of political, you know,

01:09:15.030 --> 01:09:18.360
constraints that are
affecting people's lives

01:09:18.360 --> 01:09:22.710
as well as environmental and social things

01:09:22.710 --> 01:09:23.580
that are determining

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:27.450
how many of the marginalized
communities are subsisting,

01:09:27.450 --> 01:09:28.530
if you will,

01:09:28.530 --> 01:09:31.020
that we need to incorporate those concepts

01:09:31.020 --> 01:09:32.610
into the work that we do

01:09:32.610 --> 01:09:36.900
in just promoting health in
individual and communities.

01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:39.570
And that we have to partner
with all of those elements

01:09:39.570 --> 01:09:41.070
to be able to do that.

01:09:41.070 --> 01:09:44.130
And Kathy, Gillis,

01:09:44.130 --> 01:09:47.220
yes, we were primary care
policy fellows together

01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:48.480
back in the 90s,

01:09:48.480 --> 01:09:52.050
and that's one of the things
I think is so critical for us

01:09:52.050 --> 01:09:54.960
to really start to work on as well.

01:09:54.960 --> 01:09:58.170
And thank you for this
incredible presentation.

01:09:58.170 --> 01:09:59.073
I appreciate that.

01:10:00.120 --> 01:10:00.953
- Thank you.

01:10:02.137 --> 01:10:05.387
(audience applauding)

01:10:12.210 --> 01:10:17.210
- [Richard] Hi, Richard
Jennings, class of 79 midwifery.

01:10:20.130 --> 01:10:23.610
A little bit off topic, but what do you,

01:10:23.610 --> 01:10:26.910
have you considered the role
of guaranteed annual income

01:10:26.910 --> 01:10:31.910
as a way to address poverty
in the United States?

01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:37.920
- I can take the foundation.

01:10:37.920 --> 01:10:39.870
The foundation actually
has done a lot of work

01:10:39.870 --> 01:10:44.870
on different forms of
population level interventions

01:10:45.690 --> 01:10:46.980
that can address poverty,

01:10:46.980 --> 01:10:49.530
child poverty and health improvements.

01:10:49.530 --> 01:10:52.890
And one of the shocking
things we learned years ago,

01:10:52.890 --> 01:10:57.890
was actually the most effective
healthcare access program

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:01.320
in recent American social policy history

01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:03.300
was the earned income tax credit,

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:06.150
the most, which for those of you know,

01:11:06.150 --> 01:11:11.150
is a basically cash transfer
to certain working families.

01:11:11.490 --> 01:11:15.600
The most effective by far poverty program

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:18.300
in the United States in any recent memory

01:11:18.300 --> 01:11:20.640
is the refundable child tax credit.

01:11:20.640 --> 01:11:23.700
And our foundation actually supported,

01:11:23.700 --> 01:11:27.480
we had this strategy back in 2018

01:11:27.480 --> 01:11:31.230
to support advocating for
those two interventions

01:11:31.230 --> 01:11:33.750
in red states and blue states.

01:11:33.750 --> 01:11:37.200
And so even certain states
that didn't have income tax

01:11:37.200 --> 01:11:39.930
could still have a
refundable child tax credit,

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:41.640
or earned income tax credit.

01:11:41.640 --> 01:11:43.590
And then when Covid hit,

01:11:43.590 --> 01:11:46.770
we actually brought all those
local state grantees together

01:11:46.770 --> 01:11:51.210
to Washington and had them
meet with decision makers

01:11:51.210 --> 01:11:53.280
in Congress and the White House.

01:11:53.280 --> 01:11:56.190
And of course the part
of the Covid response

01:11:56.190 --> 01:11:59.610
was the largest social policy experiment

01:11:59.610 --> 01:12:01.260
in recent American decades,

01:12:01.260 --> 01:12:04.470
which was vastly expanding
those two efforts

01:12:04.470 --> 01:12:08.850
as part of providing some form
of basic guaranteed income.

01:12:08.850 --> 01:12:11.760
And it was wildly effective,

01:12:11.760 --> 01:12:14.490
child poverty got cut in half
the minute that went away,

01:12:14.490 --> 01:12:16.500
child poverty doubled.

01:12:16.500 --> 01:12:20.430
It's just shocking how
pinpoint accurate that was.

01:12:20.430 --> 01:12:22.770
And there are a consortia of foundations

01:12:22.770 --> 01:12:24.750
that studied all the impacts

01:12:24.750 --> 01:12:27.690
and found people don't waste the money.

01:12:27.690 --> 01:12:30.720
People don't use it for
things that are not considered

01:12:30.720 --> 01:12:31.920
high value.

01:12:31.920 --> 01:12:35.850
It's mostly healthcare,
childcare, support,

01:12:35.850 --> 01:12:39.270
so families can be back in the labor force

01:12:39.270 --> 01:12:42.480
and nutrition for children.

01:12:42.480 --> 01:12:45.120
And so I lean and her teamwork every day

01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:48.390
to try to build a more
robust political environment

01:12:48.390 --> 01:12:51.600
that can look at the data
and make rational decisions.

01:12:51.600 --> 01:12:55.443
We're not quite there yet
as a country, but it is,

01:12:56.610 --> 01:12:59.340
and that's not specific
answer on basic income,

01:12:59.340 --> 01:13:02.700
but those are the biggest
experiments we have

01:13:02.700 --> 01:13:04.560
with any sort of direct cash transfer

01:13:04.560 --> 01:13:06.573
that effectively operates as such.

01:13:14.010 --> 01:13:15.690
- [Audience Member] Hi, good afternoon.

01:13:15.690 --> 01:13:17.190
Back to the wellness.

01:13:17.190 --> 01:13:19.640
I think it was the second
question about wellness

01:13:20.760 --> 01:13:25.440
in the same manner, how
do we provide incentives?

01:13:25.440 --> 01:13:27.930
Because everyone in this room,

01:13:27.930 --> 01:13:31.770
and many of us know that
wellness may be the answer,

01:13:31.770 --> 01:13:34.830
but if the finances,

01:13:34.830 --> 01:13:39.540
if we're in such a model of
disease and symptom treatment,

01:13:39.540 --> 01:13:42.360
and that's where the
money is for providers

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:45.300
and people coming out of medical school,

01:13:45.300 --> 01:13:47.370
who also have huge debt,

01:13:47.370 --> 01:13:49.140
and this is where they're
gonna make their money

01:13:49.140 --> 01:13:52.200
and politically pharmaceuticals.

01:13:52.200 --> 01:13:56.310
What are your thoughts about systemically,

01:13:56.310 --> 01:14:00.780
it's so easy to say wellness,
and I've read the data too,

01:14:00.780 --> 01:14:02.430
about preventable diseases,

01:14:02.430 --> 01:14:06.150
but what are some real
steps that could be taken

01:14:06.150 --> 01:14:09.870
to shift the structure in this country?

01:14:09.870 --> 01:14:13.440
- Hmm, well, speaking of someone

01:14:13.440 --> 01:14:15.390
who has no particular expertise on this,

01:14:15.390 --> 01:14:19.360
other than a little bit
about behavior change

01:14:20.520 --> 01:14:23.013
at the individual level, you know,

01:14:25.020 --> 01:14:27.420
it providing incentives for individuals

01:14:27.420 --> 01:14:29.610
to make lifestyle changes

01:14:29.610 --> 01:14:32.320
that are probably built into
their healthcare coverage

01:14:33.540 --> 01:14:38.100
would be one level at which to start.

01:14:38.100 --> 01:14:40.260
But, you know, when we've
tried that, for example,

01:14:40.260 --> 01:14:42.660
I don't know how many of
you are Yale Health members,

01:14:42.660 --> 01:14:45.270
but I get my healthcare from Yale Health

01:14:45.270 --> 01:14:48.267
when we've tried that,
where you get, you know,

01:14:48.267 --> 01:14:49.100
your premiums are lower,

01:14:49.100 --> 01:14:51.360
or you don't have to
pay or copay, whatever.

01:14:51.360 --> 01:14:55.800
When you agree to a set
of wellness interventions,

01:14:57.360 --> 01:14:59.430
it's hard to get people to agree.

01:14:59.430 --> 01:15:01.830
They resent the threat to their,

01:15:01.830 --> 01:15:04.860
what they believe is their
autonomy and their freedom.

01:15:04.860 --> 01:15:08.310
And I think we have to
work on that problem.

01:15:08.310 --> 01:15:11.880
And, you know, individual
level incentives are good,

01:15:11.880 --> 01:15:13.620
but maybe we should try some group

01:15:13.620 --> 01:15:18.620
and societal level incentives
to try to encourage people

01:15:18.690 --> 01:15:22.890
to want to engage in those behaviors.

01:15:22.890 --> 01:15:26.070
- You know, I could add
two interesting experiments

01:15:26.070 --> 01:15:28.830
that I think offer a path
forward. And this could be an area

01:15:28.830 --> 01:15:30.360
where we learn from other countries,

01:15:30.360 --> 01:15:33.150
but about 20 years ago in Mexico,

01:15:33.150 --> 01:15:34.860
they had a program called PROGRESA.

01:15:34.860 --> 01:15:37.260
I don't know how many of
you are familiar with that,

01:15:37.260 --> 01:15:39.960
but they offered
conditional cash transfers

01:15:39.960 --> 01:15:43.770
as President Salovey
just said to, you know,

01:15:43.770 --> 01:15:45.630
incentivize certain behaviors.

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:48.030
And they're not dramatically
large cash transfers,

01:15:48.030 --> 01:15:51.060
but they encourage, you know,

01:15:51.060 --> 01:15:54.930
a certain number of prenatal
visits, and postnatal visits,

01:15:54.930 --> 01:15:59.790
and other kinds of preventative
oriented interventions.

01:15:59.790 --> 01:16:02.220
And they were extraordinarily effective.

01:16:02.220 --> 01:16:05.910
Just, it was a behavioral
economics reality

01:16:05.910 --> 01:16:07.320
that they were extremely effective.

01:16:07.320 --> 01:16:09.750
And some of those efforts
got scaled around the world,

01:16:09.750 --> 01:16:13.110
Bangladesh, Kenya, and we saw,

01:16:13.110 --> 01:16:16.080
so we saw such significant results

01:16:16.080 --> 01:16:19.680
that we even brought some of
those to Baltimore and Detroit

01:16:19.680 --> 01:16:22.560
and places in the US where
they've also been effective,

01:16:22.560 --> 01:16:26.310
so I think there's evidence
for that basic approach.

01:16:26.310 --> 01:16:29.310
The other effort we just
started is a major partnership

01:16:29.310 --> 01:16:32.760
with the American Heart Association
called Food Is Medicine.

01:16:32.760 --> 01:16:34.020
And in that context,

01:16:34.020 --> 01:16:36.930
insurers have been supporting experiments

01:16:36.930 --> 01:16:40.830
to identify whether dietary interventions

01:16:40.830 --> 01:16:43.650
can in fact reduce
hemoglobin A one C levels

01:16:43.650 --> 01:16:47.310
in pre-diabetic and diabetic populations.

01:16:47.310 --> 01:16:50.370
And it's been so effective
that we're scaling that up

01:16:50.370 --> 01:16:54.120
and doing a big national
study to explore that

01:16:54.120 --> 01:16:57.900
as a basic approach to reducing
the financial pressure.

01:16:57.900 --> 01:17:01.400
We spend $300 billion
a year on diabetes care

01:17:01.400 --> 01:17:06.360
in the United States, nearly
all of which is preventable.

01:17:06.360 --> 01:17:09.600
And it's basically a dietary
disease, as you know.

01:17:09.600 --> 01:17:12.300
So, you know, but it is unfortunate

01:17:12.300 --> 01:17:14.220
that for all of these types of things,

01:17:14.220 --> 01:17:16.680
you're up against some reality. (laughs)

01:17:16.680 --> 01:17:18.675
And the reality we're up against is,

01:17:18.675 --> 01:17:22.620
we spend trillions of dollars every year

01:17:22.620 --> 01:17:26.190
in a very treatment intervention oriented

01:17:26.190 --> 01:17:29.910
after the fact medical
system that proved to be

01:17:29.910 --> 01:17:33.000
extremely ineffective
during the Covid pandemic

01:17:33.000 --> 01:17:35.040
in protecting Americans' lives.

01:17:35.040 --> 01:17:38.640
- Yeah, and there's so many
little demonstration experiments

01:17:38.640 --> 01:17:41.613
that we can learn from
right here in New Haven,

01:17:44.338 --> 01:17:47.320
there is a program to
encourage community gardening

01:17:48.870 --> 01:17:52.800
and it teamed up with a program
to encourage healthy eating,

01:17:52.800 --> 01:17:53.880
so that the community,

01:17:53.880 --> 01:17:55.800
when you went to your community garden,

01:17:55.800 --> 01:17:58.833
learned how to grow in an urban setting,

01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:01.890
often very urban setting.

01:18:01.890 --> 01:18:05.610
When you learned how to grow
things that you could eat,

01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:09.420
you then also learned how to
cook them in the healthiest way

01:18:09.420 --> 01:18:10.820
and what have you.

01:18:10.820 --> 01:18:12.540
So that those are very
small little interventions,

01:18:12.540 --> 01:18:17.540
very local, but that's maybe
a good place for it to begin.

01:18:18.060 --> 01:18:19.563
I'm also reading right now,

01:18:20.400 --> 01:18:23.100
many of you know our professor Tim Snyder.

01:18:23.100 --> 01:18:28.100
Tim is the world's expert
really on the history

01:18:28.440 --> 01:18:32.040
of Eastern Europe, the Baltic region,

01:18:32.040 --> 01:18:34.620
and now Russia and Ukraine.

01:18:34.620 --> 01:18:36.660
And many of you have seen him on the news

01:18:36.660 --> 01:18:39.660
and his course on the history of Ukraine

01:18:39.660 --> 01:18:44.370
is online for anybody to
take and is very, very good.

01:18:44.370 --> 01:18:49.050
People tell me that they
appreciate it all the time.

01:18:49.050 --> 01:18:51.753
But Tim wrote a little
book a couple of years ago,

01:18:53.431 --> 01:18:54.540
I think it's called "Our Malady,"

01:18:54.540 --> 01:18:57.720
and it comes out of his
experience of being a patient

01:18:57.720 --> 01:19:00.750
with a life threatening disease,

01:19:00.750 --> 01:19:02.433
or life-threatening condition.

01:19:05.280 --> 01:19:08.280
But being, that's how it starts.

01:19:08.280 --> 01:19:09.420
And these were his diaries,

01:19:09.420 --> 01:19:13.200
but then he reflects on how
care was delivered to him

01:19:13.200 --> 01:19:15.060
all over the world, 'cause
he travels the world,

01:19:15.060 --> 01:19:16.113
he and his family.

01:19:17.010 --> 01:19:18.680
And the example I just happened to read

01:19:18.680 --> 01:19:20.990
in the last couple days
was, I think it was,

01:19:20.990 --> 01:19:24.390
it was either the Netherlands or Austria,

01:19:24.390 --> 01:19:27.150
I can't remember now,
where you get a little,

01:19:27.150 --> 01:19:29.910
when you get your prenatal care,

01:19:29.910 --> 01:19:32.880
you get a little passport thing,
that looks like a passport.

01:19:32.880 --> 01:19:37.880
And every time you do a prenatal visit,

01:19:38.280 --> 01:19:41.160
it gets a stamp in the box for that visit.

01:19:41.160 --> 01:19:46.160
After you have a baby, you get
stamps for well baby visits

01:19:47.340 --> 01:19:48.173
and the like.

01:19:48.173 --> 01:19:50.790
And as you accumulate these stamps,

01:19:50.790 --> 01:19:53.100
other healthcare needs that you might have

01:19:53.100 --> 01:19:55.050
are more deeply discounted.

01:19:55.050 --> 01:19:56.010
- Yeah.
- Right?

01:19:56.010 --> 01:19:57.510
And all you have to do
is show your passport

01:19:57.510 --> 01:19:58.650
with all these stamps in them.

01:19:58.650 --> 01:20:02.700
So it doesn't require great
technological intervention,

01:20:02.700 --> 01:20:05.403
but you show your passport
if they're stamped,

01:20:06.870 --> 01:20:08.850
when you need something done
in the healthcare system,

01:20:08.850 --> 01:20:10.697
it's less expensive for you, right?

01:20:10.697 --> 01:20:13.920
It was the simplest kind of intervention.

01:20:13.920 --> 01:20:17.430
And apparently everybody
does it because it is simple.

01:20:17.430 --> 01:20:18.360
So there you have it,

01:20:18.360 --> 01:20:20.340
you're doing wellness prevention

01:20:20.340 --> 01:20:23.130
and it's getting you something
in addition to a longer,

01:20:23.130 --> 01:20:27.180
healthier life fairly immediately.

01:20:27.180 --> 01:20:29.340
- I would like to add to, you know,

01:20:29.340 --> 01:20:31.740
in addition to individual incentives

01:20:31.740 --> 01:20:35.850
that you both alluded to,

01:20:35.850 --> 01:20:37.950
I think that the systemic changes

01:20:37.950 --> 01:20:41.790
and also what Dr. Shah
suggested about really learning

01:20:41.790 --> 01:20:46.620
from the other countries
that have a much more focus

01:20:46.620 --> 01:20:49.080
on prevention and promotion

01:20:49.080 --> 01:20:53.070
that has shown how they can reduced,

01:20:53.070 --> 01:20:54.630
they have reduced their, you know,

01:20:54.630 --> 01:20:58.170
healthcare services costs
and the health outcomes

01:20:58.170 --> 01:21:00.630
of are much, much better.

01:21:00.630 --> 01:21:05.630
So I think that we need to also
focus more on policy changes

01:21:06.450 --> 01:21:10.410
on, you know, political
determinants of health.

01:21:10.410 --> 01:21:13.770
We focus a lot on social
determinants of health,

01:21:13.770 --> 01:21:17.490
but I think that what we
miss is that, you know,

01:21:17.490 --> 01:21:20.217
the root cause of a lot of, you know,

01:21:20.217 --> 01:21:23.280
poor outcomes in this country

01:21:23.280 --> 01:21:26.310
goes back to the political
determinants of health

01:21:26.310 --> 01:21:30.813
and how the decisions that
are made really, you know,

01:21:31.800 --> 01:21:36.800
prevents healthcare providers
and healthcare system to,

01:21:37.620 --> 01:21:41.640
you know, care more
about the health outcomes

01:21:41.640 --> 01:21:43.290
of all the people.

01:21:43.290 --> 01:21:46.890
And so thank you, yeah.

01:21:46.890 --> 01:21:47.723
Yes.

01:21:49.020 --> 01:21:50.400
- [Audience Member] I
wanna thank all of you

01:21:50.400 --> 01:21:52.980
for a really inspirational,

01:21:52.980 --> 01:21:56.730
your talks and your comments
about past and future.

01:21:56.730 --> 01:22:00.063
And to follow up Dr. Emami in terms of,

01:22:01.240 --> 01:22:02.850
in the passport issue,

01:22:02.850 --> 01:22:05.760
when I was doing research in the UK,

01:22:05.760 --> 01:22:09.480
all women in the UK carry their
prenatal record themselves

01:22:09.480 --> 01:22:11.550
for the entire pregnancy.

01:22:11.550 --> 01:22:14.310
And no backup, you know,

01:22:14.310 --> 01:22:16.920
it was this pre-electronic
record, but no backup.

01:22:16.920 --> 01:22:21.920
And I remember asking, as
an ethnographer, I said,

01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:24.450
well, don't they ever lose it?

01:22:24.450 --> 01:22:27.159
And they said, of course not.

01:22:27.159 --> 01:22:27.992
(audience laughing)

01:22:27.992 --> 01:22:30.030
We lose it much more than they do.

01:22:30.030 --> 01:22:33.450
And then they said, why would she lose it?

01:22:33.450 --> 01:22:36.636
That's her baby, that's her future.

01:22:36.636 --> 01:22:38.760
And so that inevitable trust in the people

01:22:38.760 --> 01:22:42.330
that we're caring for is
so incredibly important.

01:22:42.330 --> 01:22:43.163
- Yeah.

01:22:43.163 --> 01:22:44.748
- [Audience Member] But
that's not my question.

01:22:44.748 --> 01:22:45.870
(audience laughing)

01:22:45.870 --> 01:22:50.700
My question is, has been said,

01:22:50.700 --> 01:22:55.080
we spend so much money on
healthcare and yet we have,

01:22:55.080 --> 01:23:00.080
so our primary providers on
the front lines are limited.

01:23:00.720 --> 01:23:02.310
We don't have enough

01:23:02.310 --> 01:23:06.090
and we certainly don't
have enough midwives.

01:23:06.090 --> 01:23:11.090
And it's not just having
more spaces in schools,

01:23:11.520 --> 01:23:13.320
we turn away students

01:23:13.320 --> 01:23:15.450
because we don't have
enough clinical sites.

01:23:15.450 --> 01:23:18.600
And one of the reasons we don't
have enough clinical sites,

01:23:18.600 --> 01:23:20.310
is there's not an investment

01:23:20.310 --> 01:23:23.253
in this country in
graduate nursing education.

01:23:24.135 --> 01:23:26.730
It's a huge investment
in medical education.

01:23:26.730 --> 01:23:28.470
So I'd ask you to say,

01:23:28.470 --> 01:23:31.650
what could we do to upend at that?

01:23:31.650 --> 01:23:35.760
Because I think it is about
being able to help people

01:23:35.760 --> 01:23:39.690
have access to the marvelous
education in so many schools,

01:23:39.690 --> 01:23:40.840
and especially at Yale.

01:23:44.813 --> 01:23:48.063
(audience applauding)

01:23:51.626 --> 01:23:54.030
- Well, I don't know the
answer, but I'd say this,

01:23:54.030 --> 01:23:58.560
if there was ever a
moment in recent history

01:23:58.560 --> 01:24:03.560
where the basic importance
of nursing, primary care,

01:24:06.630 --> 01:24:11.630
massively scaling up diagnostic
capacity in this country,

01:24:11.850 --> 01:24:16.850
and democratizing access to
basic health interventions

01:24:17.220 --> 01:24:20.880
was seen and felt by everyone,
it was the Covid pandemic.

01:24:20.880 --> 01:24:24.060
And I don't actually know,

01:24:24.060 --> 01:24:26.250
I'm sure there's expertise in this room,

01:24:26.250 --> 01:24:28.200
but it feels like coming out of Covid,

01:24:28.200 --> 01:24:31.110
if we really look at why did America have

01:24:31.110 --> 01:24:35.130
the most excess mortality
against modeled predictions,

01:24:35.130 --> 01:24:39.330
the answer was, America
has the least investment

01:24:39.330 --> 01:24:43.320
on a relative basis in good primary care.

01:24:43.320 --> 01:24:45.150
We don't value it as much as we should.

01:24:45.150 --> 01:24:48.150
We don't pay people as much
as we should in that space.

01:24:48.150 --> 01:24:49.890
We don't build infrastructure as much.

01:24:49.890 --> 01:24:53.490
We don't even have enough testing capacity

01:24:53.490 --> 01:24:55.920
that reaches every community.

01:24:55.920 --> 01:24:59.130
And that on a relative basis

01:24:59.130 --> 01:25:02.940
is not that expensive to
fix relative to, you know,

01:25:02.940 --> 01:25:05.640
the trillions of dollars that we spend

01:25:05.640 --> 01:25:07.380
on other parts of the healthcare system.

01:25:07.380 --> 01:25:12.380
So this feels like our moment,
you know, to make that case.

01:25:16.950 --> 01:25:19.440
- Okay. Hi, I am LaRon Nelson.

01:25:19.440 --> 01:25:22.080
I am the Associate Dean of Global Affairs

01:25:22.080 --> 01:25:23.163
and Planetary Health.

01:25:24.210 --> 01:25:26.730
First wanna welcome and honor Dean Emami

01:25:26.730 --> 01:25:28.650
for accepting to lead our school

01:25:28.650 --> 01:25:31.590
as we enter into the next 100 years.

01:25:31.590 --> 01:25:32.795
Thank you.

01:25:32.795 --> 01:25:36.120
(audience applauding)

01:25:36.120 --> 01:25:38.220
I also want to thank President Salovey

01:25:38.220 --> 01:25:40.620
and Dr. Shah for being here to celebrate

01:25:40.620 --> 01:25:42.870
the centennial with us and
for offering your thoughts

01:25:42.870 --> 01:25:45.180
on the future of nursing at Yale.

01:25:45.180 --> 01:25:47.760
My question is for you, Dr. Shah,

01:25:47.760 --> 01:25:51.780
I appreciated reading your commentary

01:25:51.780 --> 01:25:53.970
that you published last summer online,

01:25:53.970 --> 01:25:57.690
which talked about the
Rockefeller's Foundation's interest

01:25:57.690 --> 01:26:01.380
in addressing the human
toll of the climate crisis.

01:26:01.380 --> 01:26:02.850
As you've already heard,

01:26:02.850 --> 01:26:05.640
it's something that we're
trying to prepare nurses,

01:26:05.640 --> 01:26:07.200
nurse midwives, nurse practitioners,

01:26:07.200 --> 01:26:08.610
scientists and leaders to contribute

01:26:08.610 --> 01:26:10.203
to that response as well.

01:26:11.250 --> 01:26:14.640
One of the things that we
also think a lot about here

01:26:14.640 --> 01:26:18.000
as it relates to the frequency
of extreme weather event

01:26:18.000 --> 01:26:21.660
goes beyond the tragic death tolls,

01:26:21.660 --> 01:26:26.010
but really to the scale of the
post-traumatic stress effects

01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:28.820
that will occur in a global mental health,

01:26:28.820 --> 01:26:31.260
in the context of global
mental health infrastructure

01:26:31.260 --> 01:26:33.660
that is brittle,

01:26:33.660 --> 01:26:35.580
maybe to say to the least

01:26:35.580 --> 01:26:38.760
in many lower middle income countries.

01:26:38.760 --> 01:26:43.740
We know that the need to
have community-wide wellness

01:26:43.740 --> 01:26:48.360
is going to be key to any
real sustainable recovery,

01:26:48.360 --> 01:26:49.800
whether it's social, political,

01:26:49.800 --> 01:26:52.440
economic and communities
across the planet.

01:26:52.440 --> 01:26:53.970
What I'd like to know,

01:26:53.970 --> 01:26:56.040
if you could offer us any
thoughts from the foundation

01:26:56.040 --> 01:26:59.310
about the role of promoting
global mental wellness

01:26:59.310 --> 01:27:01.830
as a strategy for human adaptation

01:27:01.830 --> 01:27:04.260
in the context of this
current climate crisis?

01:27:04.260 --> 01:27:06.600
- Yeah, what a great, great question.

01:27:06.600 --> 01:27:09.046
As would be expected from Yale.

01:27:09.046 --> 01:27:10.590
(audience laughing)

01:27:10.590 --> 01:27:12.750
As you know, I mean, mental health

01:27:12.750 --> 01:27:17.440
is the fastest growing source of morbidity

01:27:18.480 --> 01:27:21.990
according to the WHO on a planetary basis.

01:27:21.990 --> 01:27:25.950
And if you look specifically
at climate and the estimates,

01:27:25.950 --> 01:27:29.130
you know, you have the
number of hungry communities

01:27:29.130 --> 01:27:32.610
going from about 700 million
people to 1.4 billion people.

01:27:32.610 --> 01:27:35.550
You have 200 million climate migrants.

01:27:35.550 --> 01:27:40.410
That's a lot of disruption in
conflict and political strife

01:27:40.410 --> 01:27:44.430
and in human wellbeing.

01:27:44.430 --> 01:27:46.560
So I think in general,

01:27:46.560 --> 01:27:49.980
mental health has to be a much
bigger part of the assessment

01:27:49.980 --> 01:27:54.450
and the response kit for nations
that are trying to prepare

01:27:54.450 --> 01:27:56.760
for what their public health
infrastructure looks like

01:27:56.760 --> 01:27:57.930
in the future.

01:27:57.930 --> 01:27:59.310
Unfortunately, right now,

01:27:59.310 --> 01:28:02.670
most of the countries
that are most acutely hit

01:28:02.670 --> 01:28:06.030
are going through
effectively a debt crisis.

01:28:06.030 --> 01:28:10.320
What happened during Covid
was we poured 30 plus percent

01:28:10.320 --> 01:28:13.830
of GDP effectively and
monetary and fiscal stimulus

01:28:13.830 --> 01:28:16.500
into our economy and it created a floor.

01:28:16.500 --> 01:28:19.050
They couldn't do that in 81 countries

01:28:19.050 --> 01:28:22.595
that house most of the world's
most vulnerable people.

01:28:22.595 --> 01:28:25.440
And as a result, right
now, if you go to Kenya,

01:28:25.440 --> 01:28:28.950
62% of their public budget
is paying off interest.

01:28:28.950 --> 01:28:31.860
Ghana, I think it's 74%,

01:28:31.860 --> 01:28:34.350
and that's true in 40 plus countries.

01:28:34.350 --> 01:28:36.930
And so at a time when
they should be investing

01:28:36.930 --> 01:28:40.590
in what you're describing
and planning for the future,

01:28:40.590 --> 01:28:44.250
they're actually cutting
back on health services,

01:28:44.250 --> 01:28:46.830
which is why these
numbers are getting worse

01:28:46.830 --> 01:28:51.030
instead of better on basic
things like maternal mortality,

01:28:51.030 --> 01:28:53.730
child mortality, and
infectious disease prevalence.

01:28:53.730 --> 01:28:57.060
So there's a big financing problem

01:28:57.060 --> 01:29:00.210
and it's great to hear that
you have enough of a culture

01:29:00.210 --> 01:29:03.570
of interdisciplinary problem
solving that you can, you know,

01:29:03.570 --> 01:29:07.020
bring your expertise from here
together with policy teams

01:29:07.020 --> 01:29:09.090
that work on those issues.

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:12.690
But it clearly has to be a
very, very big part of planning,

01:29:12.690 --> 01:29:15.000
proper planning for public
health in the future

01:29:15.000 --> 01:29:19.173
has to be redefined in a
much, much broader way.

01:29:21.990 --> 01:29:24.280
- That's global and local.
- Yeah.

01:29:24.280 --> 01:29:26.913
- So if you live in New Haven, like I do,

01:29:28.189 --> 01:29:29.970
you know, a big part of the city's budget

01:29:29.970 --> 01:29:32.310
is servicing debt
- Yeah.

01:29:32.310 --> 01:29:37.310
- And making up for underfunded
pensions, pension programs.

01:29:37.560 --> 01:29:41.010
And it's only after you pay for those

01:29:41.010 --> 01:29:43.200
that you begin to think about, you know,

01:29:43.200 --> 01:29:45.000
what could we do to promote the wellness

01:29:45.000 --> 01:29:48.420
of of our population?

01:29:48.420 --> 01:29:50.280
- [Announcer] Unfortunately, we have time

01:29:50.280 --> 01:29:51.963
for only one more question.

01:29:52.950 --> 01:29:56.973
So I think I saw somebody
over here. Sorry.

01:29:57.810 --> 01:29:58.760
- Careful, careful.

01:30:00.180 --> 01:30:01.013
- [Carole] Carole McKenzie,

01:30:01.013 --> 01:30:04.800
class of 73 maternal
born and nurse Midwifery.

01:30:04.800 --> 01:30:07.140
I live in rural East Texas

01:30:07.140 --> 01:30:09.813
as somewhat unenlightened political state,

01:30:09.813 --> 01:30:10.646
(audience laughing)

01:30:10.646 --> 01:30:14.100
with horrible maternal newborn outcomes.

01:30:14.100 --> 01:30:15.990
And I guess my question is,

01:30:15.990 --> 01:30:18.870
and also advanced nurse practitioners

01:30:18.870 --> 01:30:20.670
do not have full authority.

01:30:20.670 --> 01:30:22.890
So if you look at all of those issues,

01:30:22.890 --> 01:30:24.540
how do you navigate

01:30:24.540 --> 01:30:27.450
the incredibly horrible
political landscape,

01:30:27.450 --> 01:30:30.325
without causing a political
brouhaha here, but-

01:30:30.325 --> 01:30:33.090
(audience laughing)

01:30:33.090 --> 01:30:34.735
- [Audience Member] Turn off the camera.

01:30:34.735 --> 01:30:36.530
(audience laughing)

01:30:36.530 --> 01:30:38.231
- Well, let me try.

01:30:38.231 --> 01:30:43.231
(audience laughing)
(audience applauding)

01:30:46.440 --> 01:30:49.263
Let me try, but I'll ask others to help.

01:30:50.900 --> 01:30:51.733
- [Audience Member] I
(indistinct) too later.

01:30:51.733 --> 01:30:53.010
- Yeah. Well there is,

01:30:53.010 --> 01:30:55.920
I actually think there
are potential solutions

01:30:55.920 --> 01:30:57.360
on both sides of the political aisle.

01:30:57.360 --> 01:31:02.360
So for example, and we try
and do support policy analysis

01:31:04.350 --> 01:31:08.040
and advocacy and innovation on both sides

01:31:08.040 --> 01:31:09.420
of the political aisle,

01:31:09.420 --> 01:31:14.260
but there ought to be a
movement to deregulate

01:31:15.180 --> 01:31:19.320
the medical certification rule structure,

01:31:19.320 --> 01:31:22.620
so that labor can be rewarded

01:31:22.620 --> 01:31:27.270
for the value it can generate,
right? At every level.

01:31:27.270 --> 01:31:29.220
And that's a pretty,

01:31:29.220 --> 01:31:30.810
if that from first principles,

01:31:30.810 --> 01:31:34.020
that's a fairly conservative argument.

01:31:34.020 --> 01:31:39.000
And we found some very
conservative Republican partners

01:31:39.000 --> 01:31:42.120
would advocate for that with us

01:31:42.120 --> 01:31:45.210
alongside some more
progressive communities

01:31:45.210 --> 01:31:47.280
that want to eliminate health disparities

01:31:47.280 --> 01:31:50.760
and have a different way of
coming at the same problem.

01:31:50.760 --> 01:31:52.470
And so, you know,

01:31:52.470 --> 01:31:53.907
I mentioned what I mentioned

01:31:53.907 --> 01:31:55.230
about the earned income tax credit,

01:31:55.230 --> 01:31:57.990
that was actually created by Richard Nixon

01:31:57.990 --> 01:32:00.510
in a conservative policy context

01:32:00.510 --> 01:32:04.350
as the first negative income
tax in the United States.

01:32:04.350 --> 01:32:07.470
And so shockingly there are areas

01:32:07.470 --> 01:32:09.150
where you can make progress,

01:32:09.150 --> 01:32:11.970
but they do seem to be
dwarfed by the noise

01:32:11.970 --> 01:32:15.060
and the noise is only gonna get more noisy

01:32:15.060 --> 01:32:16.800
over the course of the next year.

01:32:16.800 --> 01:32:20.310
But the underlying point you're making is,

01:32:20.310 --> 01:32:24.330
until everyone can be
freed to use their skills

01:32:24.330 --> 01:32:28.290
to help their communities be
well and be rewarded for that,

01:32:28.290 --> 01:32:30.030
and not be constrained by rules

01:32:30.030 --> 01:32:33.600
that are effectively guild structure,

01:32:33.600 --> 01:32:37.680
political fighting, you know,

01:32:37.680 --> 01:32:40.380
we're not gonna have the workforce we need

01:32:40.380 --> 01:32:42.330
unleash the way we need to do it.

01:32:42.330 --> 01:32:47.040
And frankly, I personally think
that the application of AI

01:32:47.040 --> 01:32:50.430
and other forms of technology
will put even more pressure

01:32:50.430 --> 01:32:51.780
on that system,

01:32:51.780 --> 01:32:54.570
because you'll find people who believe

01:32:54.570 --> 01:32:59.570
they uniquely own some
grand expertise will find

01:33:01.170 --> 01:33:03.720
that becomes less and less possible

01:33:03.720 --> 01:33:06.000
when your phone can do
what you used to do.

01:33:06.000 --> 01:33:07.914
- [Audience Member]
The objective arguments

01:33:07.914 --> 01:33:09.047
and the data don't seem to work.

01:33:09.047 --> 01:33:09.990
- Yeah.

01:33:09.990 --> 01:33:11.797
- [Audience Member] The Political
landscape doesn't allow,

01:33:11.797 --> 01:33:14.910
and I know in the last
hurricane in Houston,

01:33:14.910 --> 01:33:16.680
they turned away nurse practitioners

01:33:16.680 --> 01:33:19.400
because they didn't have
full practice authority.

01:33:19.400 --> 01:33:22.230
They came to help from other
states they could not practice

01:33:22.230 --> 01:33:26.170
and they were turned away
that's incredibly irresponsible.

01:33:26.170 --> 01:33:27.690
- Very, very sad.

01:33:27.690 --> 01:33:31.080
You know, I think a lot of this
is, you talked about Texas,

01:33:31.080 --> 01:33:32.010
a lot of this is due

01:33:32.010 --> 01:33:35.190
to the incredible political polarization

01:33:35.190 --> 01:33:37.740
that we see in our country right now.

01:33:37.740 --> 01:33:42.420
Some of the most important
legislative approaches

01:33:42.420 --> 01:33:46.500
to healthcare delivery, broadly speaking,

01:33:46.500 --> 01:33:48.030
were bipartisan efforts.

01:33:48.030 --> 01:33:53.030
I think who in the Senate
sponsored the Family Leave Act?

01:33:53.550 --> 01:33:56.040
Who were the two main co-sponsors

01:33:56.040 --> 01:33:57.933
of the original Family Leave Act?

01:33:58.860 --> 01:34:00.030
Did everybody remember?

01:34:00.030 --> 01:34:00.930
- [Audience member] Kennedy?

01:34:00.930 --> 01:34:04.769
- Ted Kennedy, that's one.
Who was the other one?

01:34:04.769 --> 01:34:07.410
Orrin Hatch?

01:34:07.410 --> 01:34:08.243
Okay, right?

01:34:08.243 --> 01:34:11.550
So you had one of the most
liberal members of the Senate,

01:34:11.550 --> 01:34:13.920
one of the most conservative
members of the Senate.

01:34:13.920 --> 01:34:16.290
Why do they come together and do that?

01:34:16.290 --> 01:34:18.420
First of all, they were friends.

01:34:18.420 --> 01:34:20.160
They actually could have
a civil conversation

01:34:20.160 --> 01:34:21.660
with each other.

01:34:21.660 --> 01:34:25.380
Second of all they saw
in Family Leave Act,

01:34:25.380 --> 01:34:27.990
Kennedy probably saw different things

01:34:27.990 --> 01:34:32.990
that produce the same ends or
were produced by the same end.

01:34:33.030 --> 01:34:37.110
Kennedy sees a safety net
program guaranteeing jobs,

01:34:37.110 --> 01:34:39.660
Hatch sees family friendly legislation,

01:34:39.660 --> 01:34:42.600
guaranteeing parent is gonna be home

01:34:42.600 --> 01:34:47.600
at least in those early months
or weeks of a child's life.

01:34:47.970 --> 01:34:51.900
And so they both can
get behind this program.

01:34:51.900 --> 01:34:53.070
Why don't we see that today?

01:34:53.070 --> 01:34:54.750
There's a lot of reasons
why we don't see that today.

01:34:54.750 --> 01:34:58.860
Two of them are gerrymandered
political districts

01:34:58.860 --> 01:35:00.870
that make everybody worried

01:35:00.870 --> 01:35:01.950
that they're gonna be challenged

01:35:01.950 --> 01:35:04.320
by somebody who's more extreme

01:35:04.320 --> 01:35:07.080
in their district than they are,

01:35:07.080 --> 01:35:09.240
'cause that's who votes in primaries.

01:35:09.240 --> 01:35:11.343
And so they run to the extremes,

01:35:12.300 --> 01:35:14.340
reject overtures by the other party,

01:35:14.340 --> 01:35:15.300
'cause it looks weak

01:35:15.300 --> 01:35:18.510
and because they worry
about their electability,

01:35:18.510 --> 01:35:21.270
and maybe the way in
which media is polarized,

01:35:21.270 --> 01:35:23.640
and every issue gets a,

01:35:23.640 --> 01:35:28.640
has a, you're either force
or against us kind of quality

01:35:28.830 --> 01:35:31.650
to the analysis that we hear.

01:35:31.650 --> 01:35:32.700
Maybe that will change.

01:35:32.700 --> 01:35:35.160
I think we as members of the public

01:35:35.160 --> 01:35:38.250
have to kind of demand
that kind of change.

01:35:38.250 --> 01:35:41.490
But I think we'll see more
of the kind of legislation

01:35:41.490 --> 01:35:44.463
that has led to programs that,

01:35:46.000 --> 01:35:49.350
of the kind we would all
like in the health area

01:35:49.350 --> 01:35:51.712
if both parties could get behind them,

01:35:51.712 --> 01:35:54.570
'cause there is something for
both parties in most of them.

01:35:54.570 --> 01:35:55.860
- Yeah.

01:35:55.860 --> 01:35:58.950
- And I would like to
add that I think that's,

01:35:58.950 --> 01:36:02.550
although the divisive of the, you know,

01:36:02.550 --> 01:36:06.420
political climate right
now in this country,

01:36:06.420 --> 01:36:09.300
we shouldn't underestimate the power

01:36:09.300 --> 01:36:12.210
of community organization and advocacy.

01:36:12.210 --> 01:36:13.950
I think that a lot of changes,

01:36:13.950 --> 01:36:15.990
transformational changes
that have happened

01:36:15.990 --> 01:36:18.300
throughout the history
of the United States

01:36:18.300 --> 01:36:22.500
and also other countries, if you look back

01:36:22.500 --> 01:36:24.690
they are resulted of the
community engagement,

01:36:24.690 --> 01:36:27.000
that community organization

01:36:27.000 --> 01:36:31.260
and that I would like to
look at our students here

01:36:31.260 --> 01:36:34.740
because I think that nurses
could play an important role.

01:36:34.740 --> 01:36:39.210
Part of our job as a nurse is advocacy.

01:36:39.210 --> 01:36:43.050
And then we need to really
learn more to be engaged

01:36:43.050 --> 01:36:45.480
and to really be the voice of people

01:36:45.480 --> 01:36:47.340
who are not having the voice.

01:36:47.340 --> 01:36:50.117
And that could also impact the change

01:36:50.117 --> 01:36:52.500
in the political climate of this country.

01:36:52.500 --> 01:36:56.100
So that is something that
I would like to see more

01:36:56.100 --> 01:37:01.100
in our nursing profession and
our next generation of nurses

01:37:01.770 --> 01:37:04.923
will definitely do that for us.

01:37:05.872 --> 01:37:06.705
- For sure.

01:37:06.705 --> 01:37:09.955
(audience applauding)

01:37:14.705 --> 01:37:15.538
- First of all,

01:37:15.538 --> 01:37:17.130
I would like to thank our special guests

01:37:17.130 --> 01:37:20.730
for a very wide ranging rich conversation

01:37:20.730 --> 01:37:23.010
and thank you very much the audience

01:37:23.010 --> 01:37:28.010
for your thought provoking
questions and your engagement.

01:37:29.190 --> 01:37:30.180
Thank you very much.

01:37:30.180 --> 01:37:32.130
I think that we all learned a lot

01:37:32.130 --> 01:37:37.130
and we are all inspired after
this very rich conversation.

